At the end of the trading, the Prague Stock Exchange was minus.
The Prague Stock Exchange corrected its losses after a rapid fall in the morning.
Transactions with the shares of the Czech Energy Industry Entreprechasing Company accounted for nearly half of their usual daily trading.
The Prague Stock Exchange resumed its fall from the beginning of Tuesday’s trading on Monday, when it fell by almost six percent.
The fall in Wall Street’s shares is behind the decline.
This is how the market’s reaction to the results of the U.S. Chamber of Deputies Voting is manifested, which refused to support the stabilization plan of the financial sector.
Detection of Shares in Asia
The Asian stock exchanges experienced a dramatic fall in stocks on Tuesday, even though some of the losses were eventually canceled throughout the day.
Hong Kong’s Hang Seng stock market index reported almost four percent during trading, but later canceled some of the losses and reduced the decline to about 2.5 percent.
On the Hong Kong stock exchange, the Sound Seng China Enterprises Index, which monitors Chinese shares, fell 3.8 percent, and the markets in Shanghai were closed.
Shares on the Sydney Stock Exchange lost more than five percent, but losses declined to 4.3 percent.
The Taiwan Stock Exchange fell by 3.6 percent, according to its index.
“The timing of the U.S. rescue mission is insecure and affects the world’s financial markets,” said Joseph Yam, head of Hong Kong’s Finance Council.
Hong Kong, albeit as a part of China, determines its monetary policy on its own, regardless of China’s central bank.
In Hong Kong, interest rates are at the same level as that of the United States.
According to Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd, U.S. legislatures should immediately reschedule negotiations and accept the financial system’s aid proposal.
By the way, it threatens that other countries will feel the consequences.
Bloodbath of U.S. Stock
On Monday, the U.S. House of Representatives declined a plan to support the financial system, for which 700 billion dollars (nearly 12 trillion crowns) would have had to be used.
Legislators have thus ignored President George Bush’s call to support the plan.
According to Bush, the draft would solve the main causes of the financial crisis and help stabilize the entire economy.
On Monday, U. U.S. stocks underwent a bloodbath, with the leading stock indices registering the largest fall in 20 years.
The Dow Jones fell by almost seven per cent and had a similar drop was last in 1987.
The index fell even before the vote, but as soon as it turned out that the proposal had not been approved in the House of Representatives, the index started to fall free.
Congress withdrew: The U.S. government will be able to pump 700 billion dollars into banks
The supreme leaders of the U.S. Congress and the government of President George W. Bush have reached an agreement in a broader form and financial assistance for the U.S. financial system.
They will vote on this at the beginning of next week.
U.S U.S. lawmakers have made a breakthrough in the talks on the approval of a bailout for the U.S. financial system of USD 700 billion (approximately 12 trillion crowns).
But there's no victory yet.
The congress will have to complete some details of the agreement before they can publish the final form of the law and their votes.
The draft financial system subsidisation will be discussed in the House of Representatives on Monday.
Barney Frank, Chairman of the Committee on the Financial Supervisory Authority, told Reuters on Sunday.
According to Senate sources, it is likely that the draft is expected to be voted on Wednesday as soon as possible.
According to economists, there should be an announcement that the rescue plan will be approved, which is the first important psychological factor for the revitalisation of financial markets.
However, due to the complexity of the mechanisms, “sound sobriety” will then take place.
Paulson: The plan has to be effective.
“We took a big step.
“We’ve unveiled our divergent views on the form of a market stabilisation package,” Democrat Nancy Pelosi told the Bloomberg agency.
According to him, the final vote could take place on Sunday.
Legislators met with U.S. Treasury Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson on Saturday night to give a final form to a government fund.
This would have to acquiring unsalellable mortgage-activities from financial corporations, putting financial corporations into serious losses and jeopardising the stability of the whole system.
“We are close to the final agreement on a draft that will work and that should also be effective on the market.
“The rejuvenation plan still needs to be continued, but I think we’re there,” Paulson said.
Indication for Asian trading
The global financial crisis has a significant impact on the stock markets, which fall violently.
According to Democratic senator Harry Reid of Nevada, lawmakers are therefore striving to reach an agreement in Congress on Sunday.
They want to reassure investors before trading in the Asian financial markets, where, due to the time zone, Congress’s decision would be the first to influence Monday’s trade.
However, it is not yet clear when the two chambers of the U.S. Congress will vote on the law, as well as whether the negotiations cannot be found at the last minute due to a problem.
Legislators are hoping that the law will be approved in the next few days.
However, the law is still undergoing a number of changes.
The total amount intended to assist the system shall be divided into two parts.
According to President George Bush’s request, the first USD 350 billion should be available as soon as possible.
Congress, however, could block the issuance of the remainder, set at an additional ?100 billion dollars, which was later set at an additional USD 100 billion, if you feel that the program fails to fulfill its function.
Bush Reveased Moving to Negotiations
Although the President may veto this resolution, Congress may overrule its veto.
However, in spite of these changes, the basic idea of the program remained, which was the acquisition of financial instruments for the purchase of bad mortgage securities that lost their value, as hundreds of thousands of Americans are unable to repay their mortgages.
“Everything has been worked out.
“Republic of Representatives should be able to vote on the law on Sunday, and the senificial would have to vote on Monday,” Republican Senator Judd Gregg said.
U.S. President Bush is also pleased.
A spokeswoman for President Tony Fratto stated that the president was pleased with Saturday’s progress and appreciated “ bipartisan efforts to stabilize our money markets and protect our economy.”
Restrictions on deep severance payments
Christopher Dodd, the chairman of the Senate’s banking committee who led the talks, said the lawmakers had reached an agreement on responsibility for the financial program, the protection of taxpayers from losses, the closure of discounts and the timing of the financing plan.
The new law also limits the so-called huge severance payments of the directors participating in the program and establishes a committee supervising the fund.
The fund should be managed by the Treasury Department.
“We’ve been working very hard on that and we’ve made a big step forward towards an agreement that will work and that will be useful to all Americans,” Paulson said.
The plan also takes into account the help of homeowners who have problems with repayment.
The government would have to negotiate new instalments on the mortgages they have purchased, with the aim of reducing the monthly instalments of the debtors and allowing them to keep their houses.
Marek: The Spiritual Help
David Marek, Chief Economist at Patria Finance, said the proposed draft would be a good idea, but it will be very difficult to implement.
“It’s because you don’t know what the problematic assets will be bought and at what price.
“It’s going to be a decisive factor in how the financial sector and the government will alleviate the losses,” Marek told iHNed.cz this week.
According to Marek, it doesn’t matter so much that the plan will be endorsed now or at the beginning of next week.
“It’s much more important for Congress to decide this week to support the plan and give their blessing as soon as possible.
“It’s the spiritual significance of reaching an agreement that the plan will be approved is the most important thing at the moment,” Marek said.
Pygmy rafruit: MP3 players versus mobiles.
Let us help you how to choose
“While almost every mobile can play MP3 files, you can’t make a phone with a single MP3 player.
This makes it clear it’s better to just buy a phone.
Nevertheless, there are a lot of reasons to get a separate MP3 player.
The choice depends entirely on the method of use and the needs of the prospective owner.
It’s probably hard to get a high-quality expensive turntable at home to listen to the latest Maxim Turbulenc.
On the one hand, such musical products do not appear on vinyl plates, but there is no objective reason for this from a qualitative point of view either.
However, if its shelves are full of art rock, jazz or blues vinyl records, you’re probably dreaming of a turntable like this.
It’s a similar thing that’s intended for travel, that is, comcompressed—simplified with MP3 music.
There are also price and quality categories.
For some, it may be inadvisable to release 8,000 crowns for a player, just as a Pink Floyd fan probably finds it difficult to accept a 20-crowned market earphone.
There are two extremists between which there is enough room for everyone.
There's a wide selection.
The lifespan of batteries also counts.
Some high-end music players are capable of much more than simply playing the music, but also for which most mobile phones are by no accidental.
Not a single device is as universal as the phone.
In addition to the primary functions, making phone calls and sms, you can also play music and video, you can also play music and video, you can record both, have a better or worse camera in it, and you can even even navigate.
And if we take into account smartphones and communicators, he'll have a small computer in his pocket.
It’s a small miracle, as no refrigerator can grill and no television can take photos.
There are also disadvantages for multi-purpose mobile phones.
The more the functions you use on your mobile, the more the screen sends it to the charger.
Music is one of the biggest energy-educators.
Few mobile devices are able to play music for more than ten to twelve hours, usually much less.
In this regard, music players (especially the simplest ones) are significantly more powerful.
For low-income mobile phones, you should be counted that they won't play for you for more than a few hours.
In addition, if you even make a phone call, get a charger for your workplace just in case - this also applies to more expensive appliances.
There will not be such a problem for MP3 players, even the worse ones can withstand at least 9 hours of continuous operation on a single charge.
The next issue arises when uploading music to your mobile phone.
“Unfortunately, only a limited part of the devices has a universal USB socket and not all of them can be connected directly to the computer without special software.
Of course, the music can be fastened directly to the memory card, but there is only a few card readers without an adaptor with miniature memory cards used in mobile phones.
With a few exceptions (iPod) you don’t need any special software.
It's enough to recharge the device from the computer with music via USB.
The majority of mobile phones and players already create a music library that is ranked according to the name of the artist himself, the name of the album, the genre, or, for example, by the user’s assessment.
Some of the devices only represent a fast-sucpour similar to Windows Explorer, and a few can both (iriver, Samsung, Sony, Cowon, smart phones operating systems).
The players are easier to use, the mobile phones can do more
When selecting the specific equipment, be inquired primarily about battery life, the capacity of the memory, the quality and type of sound output, and of course about the comfort of the operation.
If the music primarily serves you as a back-of-the-counter and listen only for a short period of time per day, for example, any MP3-playing mobile phone will also suit you.
This can be done by any kind of mobile.
You don’t even have to pay special attention to what kind of musical formats the mobile or player can use.
The MP3 will be copied and the vast majority of the music will be in this format.
The MP3 is one of the most common, and the vast majority of devices also cope with WMA music.
AAC (format used by the Apple company and on your iPod) is less common.
For example, better mobile phones also make it possible to buy music online.
It is enough to visit the corresponding pages of the service provider (or other service provider) through the mobile internet and to buy the song or the album.
This service in itself can be a powerful argument for the purchase of music mobile.
“Unfortunately, the offer of service providers is lousy and is usually confined only to annual radio hits.
The prices aren't the lowest either.
Generally, however, it can be assumed that this method of selling music (either on GSM, on the internet or via the wireless internet) will be dominated in the future.
The majority of the people keep their mobile devices at all the time.
At any time you want to listen to something, you feel your phone in your pocket, while the forgotten MP3 player is lying at home.
However, it’s even a matter of whether you always carry your earphones or at least the hands-free ones.
If you don’t, you can use the built-in loudspeaker, with which you have almost every mobile but a small number of MP3 players.
For a lot of people, the biggest advantage of music mobile phones will probably be the price.
The cheapest ones usually cost less than 3,000 kroner.
Between three and 4,000 krones, you'll be able to get a pretty decent walkman.
Most of the time, however, you are limited by the lack of the output of the traditional earphones.
The best and most technically equipped telephones can cost up to 15,000 kroner with conventional 3.5 mm-diameter-seat ear-to-ear.
At the same time, the advantage of the players is ease of use.
The simplest – in no case, it’s necessary to deal with the cheapest players (see our assortment), everyone can really cope.
Minimal function, handling similar to the previously usual cassette cunector or gramophone is obvious.
There is no risk of collapsing in the form of an accidentally switched-enabled mobile internet, and there is no huge invoice for data transmission.
What kind of device to choose
The cheapest mobile phones (e.g. Vodafone 527) usually have an opening for removable memory cards (M2, microSD type, etc.).
You can also buy the appropriate card into a maximum of two thousand kroner.
But be cautious.
The basic types only support cards with a maximum capacity of up to 2GB.
Only the predominants can deal with up to 8 GB of memory cards.
If you already have a mobile phone and even want to buy a cheap MP3 player, choose Cowont, Creative, Sony, Samsung, Emgeton or irivert.
On the basis of the capacity (max. 2 GB), you do not have to spend more than 1,100 kroner.
If it belongs to more demanding students, choose special mobile pamen (Sony Ericsson, some Nokia and Samsungs).
These devices also have a separate MP3 control knob, thus the comfort of the treatment is approximated to the MP3 player.
The deepest in the sector is Sony Ericsson, which has the same user interface for its walkman-volutionary mobile phones, such as MP3 players sold separately.
The MP3 players of the above-mentioned brands can provide the same service in the same price range up to 4,000 crowns, but with a higher memory capacity (up to 16 GB) and battery life (usually 15 hours, up to 35 hours).
The first virtual service provider will make it to the Czech Republic this year.
Maybe it's okay.
The mobile market in the Czech Republic is relatively developed and our service providers offer practically the same services as their Western European counterparts.
Nonetheless, there’s something we’re missing here – a virtual service provider that compensates the classic service providers to significantly reduce prices.
Maybe it won't be too long.
On Mobil.cz we’ve already written many times that Czech service providers are among the most expensive in Europe.
By the way, this was reinforced, which drew attention to the fact that in the Czech Republic the price is more expensive than the other countries of the European Union.
After its last move, the regulation of roaming tariffs may result in a contradictory situation – some of the customers of Czech mobile service operators will be cheaper than domestic ones.
How is the prices in the Czech Republic so high compared to Europe?
The answer’s simple, there’s a lack of competitor who really wants to fight.
This may sound in contradiction, as we have three cellular networks and our penetration is far above 100%.
On the contrary, mobile service providers are happy to complain that the Czech market is extremely competitive.
The President of the Council of the Czech Teletechnical Office of the Czech Teletechnical Office often agrees with this, who has repeatedly stated that there is no room for a new service provider in the Czech Republic.
Perhaps it’s true that the classic service provider probably doesn’t have a lot of odds with their own network (in any case, it’s probably hard to find a frequency).
But there’s one more option – a virtual service provider.
It was precisely the entry of virtual service providers into the mobile market that resulted in a significant price decrease, after which classic service providers also had to react.
At the same time, even before the entry of large fish into the market, such as Tesco Mobile or Virgin Mobile in Great Britain, voices similar to ours were heard.
However, it has been proven that such service providers have a place and can really be successful.
We don’t have to go too far for an example.
In February of this year we informed you about the launch of Mobilking virtual service provider in Poland.
In less than six months, this service provider managed to acquire more than 100,000 customers and become the fifth-largest service provider.
At the same time, Mobilking is backed by the Penta financial group, which in the Czech Republic also owns U:fon.
The advantage of virtual service providers is the ability to focus on a much narrower target group.
In order to operate efficiently, there are far fewer customers than the traditional service provider.
It does not entail relatively high construction, cellular network management and maintenance costs.
Virtual service providers in the world generally appear as low-cost costs – they offer cheap calls and text messages, which is often counterbalanced with fewer services.
For example, Tesco Mobile in the United Kingdom offers at least 50 per cent cheaper than its competitor.
But, as the example of Mobilking in Poland shows, there can be a pretty good move for a target group.
This service provider focuses specifically on men.
“True men choose Mobilking – anyone they can trust,” says David Seibert, CEO of Mobil Entertainment Company.
It’s hard to say whether an idea similar to Poland can work for us.
A spokeswoman for Vodafone, Miroslav epick, has doubts: “The Czech market has a relatively small segmental approach on which the majority of virtual service providers are based.
In addition, it is relatively satisfying in terms of the supply of services."
There is no chance in the Czech Republic, according to mobile operators
However, it seems almost certain that if there is a virtual service provider in the Czech Republic, it will not be Mobilking.
“We are talking about the successful expansion of this project, but negotiations in the Czech Republic don’t seem to be too hopeful.
The launch of Mobilking will take place in other countries of our region,” said Jana Studnisková, a spokeswoman for the financial group Penta in a telephone interview with Mobil.cz.
This indirectly demonstrated that existing mobile operators in the Czech Republic are not very sympathetic to this idea.
On the other hand, they try to do their utmost to ensure that there is no potential competitor.
The GTS Novera group hasn’t concealed its interest in launching a virtual service provider for some time, which aims to take another step to make Telefonica O2 a real competitor in the jersey of corporate customers.
“We are very much interested in providing our customers not only with fixed-widred but also mobile telecommunications services.
The sooner, the better.
We have renegotiated this possibility with all Czech mobile operators.
Some of them have already made progress in the negotiations,” said Pavel Kaidl, a spokeswoman for the group.
There might be a better time.
From unofficial information, it seems that the first virtual service provider can be lucky until the end of the year.
For the time being, however, his birth is surrounded by an impenetrrent of impenetuable silence.
“At the moment we don’t think there is room for a full-fledged virtual service provider on the Czech market.
Contrary to other markets, the Czech market is very special.
So far we have not been approached by an unified subject offering a complex and serious project in this field,” Vlastimil Sr?e, the press department of Telefónica 02, told Mobil.cz.
The other service providers also refer to the special situation of the Czech market.
Martina Kemrová, a spokeswoman for T-Mobile, said: “Until now, we haven’t recorded a viable and bi-participating business project on which it could be refinanced.”
He added: “In the T-Mobile network, by the end of the year it is unlikely that any virtual service provider will appear.
“At the moment, there should be a concrete agreement on our table to think in such a short-term.”
Vodafone’s attention will focus on fulfilling its own 3G license requirements.
Miroslav adds: “We are open to negotiations with potential virtual service providers, primarily in the area of sharing costs and infrastructure.
This is exactly what we consider to be the most profitable from the customer’s point of view.
Vodafone’s main strategy in this area is partnering to build networks shared for fast mobile internet.”
Responsible offices - on one string with service providers
The procedure of mobile operators can be easily understandable, the entry of a new service provider would represent an increase in the competitor would lead to a decrease in prices and a decrease in their profits.
Therefore, why make it easier for such a competitor?
“When it’s very important for service providers, they’re able to work together, there’s also a grievance about the birth of the U:fon.
The greatest pressure on T-Mobile was probably about the birth of T-Mobile’s U:fon.
This is witnessed by publicly available information on the website.
But at the moment the U:fon comes in hand as a good argument as an acquisponding about why we should not allow the virtual service provider to be logged in.
Martina Kemrová added: “The environment in the competition of the mobile market has recently been impracticed with the market of the fourth mobile operator.
In view of the nature of the mobile market in the Czech Republic, it is obvious that a new player’s access to the market and its services would impose high demands.”
However, in view of such an attitude of mobile service providers, there is astonishing the complete indifference of Czech regulators.
At the same time, it’s not just about the attitude of “TOUR”, which in the Czech Republic unfortunately – unlike Great Britain and other developed countries – does not have the right to interfere in economic competition.
Only the GVH is entitled to do so.
However, the office has a characteristic attitude towards the whole problem.
“It would only be possible to enter the mobile market of the virtual service provider after a contract between the mobile service provider and a virtual service provider on the leasing of the necessary infrastructure.
Until now, no such contract has been concluded, as the offer of potential interested parties was probably uninteresting for traditional service providers,” said Kristián Chlapua, the Foreign Relations Department of the Hungarian Competition Authority.
From the representatives of the service providers, a disclaimed statement in this way would probably not surprise anyone.
However, from an office that should represent the interests of the consumers and make sure that no one can misuse their market position, this is more than strange.
Especially if Kristián Chalupa adds: “In the outskirts where virtual service providers usually operate, we can observe that their entry into the market has contributed to a decrease in the prices of mobile phone services.”
It is most likely, however, the interests of ÚOHS do not fall under the pressure for the correct functioning of the market environment and a decrease in prices.
In this case, it is really shocking to have a very touching consistency from the reaction of the three mobile operators.
The question of whether it’s just a coincidence, it’s almost self-evident.
Just as at the filling stations the same gasoline prices are probably just the works of coincidence and hard competition.
“The office did not discover signs of anti-competitive proceedings in this case, so there is no reason to interfere in any way.
None of the service providers have a managerial position, i.e. they have a comparatively greater responsibility for their market activities.
“The tools necessary to open the mobile market of virtual service providers are likely to be lacking for the telecommunications regulator,” Chalupa said.
Demonstration is one of many controversial points.
Of course, there is an analyzing of the relevant markets developed by TOUR, according to which telecommunications is the only market from the point of view of economic competition.
Of course, there is not a single Czech service provider in a leading position in such a market.
However, some legal experts also offer a different approach - for the creation of a virtual service provider it is always obligatory to approve the operator of the network.
And the mobile operator, relatively logically, has a monopoly on its own network.
It is necessarily dominant in a certain segment.
But even if we didn’t accept this argument, ÚOHS would have to be at least a reminder that three cellular operators are remarkably similar.
In the meantime, complaints inquirers (including the aforementioned GTS Novera company) show that there are very similar hindrances during negotiations with all service providers.
Responsibilities are a relatively common trick in telecommunications, with which ÚOHS takes his hand off the whole affair.
We've already encountered this in some similar situations.
“Unfortunately in the text of the recently adopted Telecommunication Act, the expansion of its powers in the field of economic competition could not be carried out in telecommunications, even though a number of experts hoped for an improvement in the situation.
Representatives of ÚOHS need to know this well, but they probably have different interests.
It works when no one listens
In the meantime, the operation of pseudo-virtual service providers remains unnoticed, of which there are already a number of people already operating in Czech mobile networks.
These are companies issuing their own cell phone service price lists, which, however, are only partially public.
We are talking about multi-level marketing companies.
For example, Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and many others offer their own mobile phone services.
The price lists of these companies usually only get to the chosen ones.
The magic lies in the fact that the company acts as a classic corporate customer against the service provider, which orders a large amount of SIM cards.
As a privileged customer, you will then receive special quotations (and other discounts).
The problem is that the commercial conditions of all Czech mobile operators prohibit similar pre-purchase.
“If there were cases that you are talking about, there would be a breach of contractual terms that we would be dealing with and which could lead to an end to cooperation with that company,” Martina Kemrová said.
But it's quite unlikely that the news of this kind of activity didn't reach the responsible people of mobile operators.
In the case of the three mentioned companies, there may be thousands of customers.
In the meantime, no service provider defends themselves against such a procedure.
They do not have negligible profits from the activities of these companies, and in the meantime – unlike real virtual service providers – they are not particularly threatened with their own official marketing.
“Miroslav “epick” warns: “The law enables similar speculation, however, the services of these companies are used only by a negligible percentage of customers.
In addition, they have to accept the fact that the SIM card is not in their name in their phones, so they cannot fully dispose of it, such as the general customers of mobile operators.”
That’s exactly what the Way should at least be strange.
There’s also another question for ÚOHS.
If mobile service providers turn a blind eye to a similar procedure, why do they hinder the birth of the virtual service provider?
It should be added that ÚOHS has a relatively strong power to expose such an activity, and if you don’t want, you don’t have to be content with the service providers’ explanation that they didn’t suspect anything like that, and that their commercial conditions are banning it.
When we don't become the most expensive
“As it has already been said a few times in the text, virtual service providers’ entry into the mobile market has resulted in an appreciable price decrease in services.
At the same time, in virtually every country where virtual service providers were established, the classic service providers tried more or less to prevent their creation.
The arguments they used were practically the same - a market that was too small, possibly too satisfied, problems with the quality of the services, etc.
In the developed countries, however, the regulatory bodies intervened precisely at this moment, which in no way protected the interests of mobile service providers, but of their customers.
Then, either through formal measures or informal pressure, they achieved the creation of the first virtual service provider that others followed.
“Unfortunately, the Czech regulatory bodies are serving the whole matter to each other.
On the basis of our experience, we cannot get rid of the impression that unambiguous competencies come in hand in these cases.
Don't give up the fight.
If the background information is good and the virtual service provider will really soon be available with us, the Czech mobile market may be put in motion again.
If one of the service providers would allow the establishment of a virtual mobile operator, ÚOHS would be difficult to remain indifferent to rejection (discrimination).
Compared to the rest of Europe, we may no longer be able to make a more communicative call.
There are four new versions of the Czech national anthem.
Please select the best
Discontinue four new versions of the Czech national anthem.
Prime Minister Mirek Topolánek solemnly premiered on the Day of the Czech State’s Office the new recordings of the state anthe-Ham? state anthem.
The anthem was recorded by the orchestra of the National Theatre, led by Ji?í B?lohlávek.
There are four versions available - with men's or female solo, chouldar or instrumentally.
They're sung by Katesina Knaínková and Adam Plachetka.
Modification of the anthem is composed of Otakar Jeremica and Jaroslav Krasek.
You can listen to all four versions on iHNed.cz
The Czech national song is one of the most beautiful hymns.
He had already told the Czech Radio that he deliberately selected young-representative-sounding singers, which are expressive and popular.
“From the very beginning, I tried to pay attention to the pure diction, the clarity of the text and the harmony,” he said.
The Czech national anthem celebrates its 175th anniversary next year.
The song “Where is my country?” was first performed in 1834 in Josef Kajetán’s Tyl Fidlovaka, complemented by the music of Frantisek Skroup.
It became popular, and people spontaneously considered it their own.
After the formation of Czechoslovakia in 1918 it became a natural part of the national anthem after the formation of Czechoslovakia.
Even after the formation of an independent Czech Republic, it remained one of the seven state symbols.
The "new" an anthem
The Prague transport company will deliver a chip card for the first time on Wednesday
Wednesday, October 1, 2008 enters itself in the history of the Prague Transport Company when the first passenger enters metros, trams and buses with an electronic coupon in his/her pocket instead of a paper ticket.
Approximately 100,000 can currently be procured by about 100,000 people who have the Opencard chip card.
Passengers will be able to purchase a 30-day and 90--day and annual coupon (which will be valid from 1 January 2009 from 1st January 2009) and at the same time you will be able to save it on their chip cards in the information centers and branches of the Transport Company’s information centers and branches at Muzeum, M?tek, Nádra?í
The coupons can already be purchased online from yesterday through the www.dpp.cz website of the Transport Company.
The service was used by more than four hundred customers during the afternoon.
Those who are starting to use internet trading can finally forget the publishing windows of the metro stations, where so far they have had to cover long queues for the line tickets on a regular basis.
The coupon can be purchased over the internet and the Opencard card is saved in one of the thirteen branches of the City Library.
“In these places, the occupants will be at the disposal of a desk with trained servers, who will help those who are interested in online purchases and will also save their purchased coupons on their cards,” said Martin Opatrina, a spokesman for the project.
In addition to the directories, electronic coupons can also be saved for Opencard in self-serving vending machines installed in some follicles.
What do these vending machines look like?
Reminiscent of the ticket-champions, and the passengers may have already spotted them at Roztyly, Bud?jovic, I. Pavlova, Florenc and Vltavská metro stations.
“Currently, there are five of them in the city, but until the end of the year there should be eighty,” said Opatrn.
The coupon is controlled by an automaton instead of the driver
The operation of the vending vending machines starting tomorrow is easy.
It is only enough to insert the Opencard chip card and a previously paid coupon will be saved on its own.
The electronic coupons are valid not only in the capital, but also in its surroundings from tomorrow.
According to trains and bus lines around the city from 300 to 499, with which the inhabitants of the districts of Central Bohemia travel mainly to the capital.
“On trains classified in Prague, the conductors were equipped with card readers, with the help of which it will also be used to determine the zone validity of the coupons,” he explained to the control of Filip Drápal from the Ropid, which compiled the time-plan of public transport in Prague.
In the buses around the city, the card readers are installed at the cash desk.
At the time of boarding, the driver not only has to be shown the chip card, but the Opencard must also be placed in a specific location on the cash desk’s side.
That’s how the driver checks the validity of the coupons.
“If, for example, the passenger needs to verify the validity of the coupons, the simplest thing to do is to ask the driver to do so,” he added.
