Olmert would give back almost all occupied territories in exchange for peace.
Ehud Olmer, the president of the Provisional Israeli Government, spoke about the return of virtually all areas occupied in 1967, the west coast of Jordan, East Jerusalem and the Golan Plateau in exchange for peace with the Palestinians and Syria.
He said this in an interview, which was published daily by the Jedi Achron on a daily basis on Monday.
“We have to reach an agreement with the Palestinians, which means that we have to give back almost all (observed) territories,” Olmer told the newspaper.
“We will keep a certain percentage of these areas, but the Palestinians will have to hand over a similar percentage of Israeli territory, because without it there will be no peace,” he added.
He acknowledged that the possible reinstatement also includes East Jerusalem.
“The one who wants safety in Jerusalem and, above all, wants tractors and bulldozers not to pass through his feet, as happened with one of my best friends, he must give up a part of Jerusalem,” remarked, referring to the attack on two July cities in July, in which a few people were killed and a few dozen were injured.
According to the Prime Minister, it will be necessary to find a special solution to the “temperature-chap, holy and historical places in the Old Town”.
“Those who want to preserve Israel’s sovereignty over the entire city must integrate 270,000.
“It won’t work,” he emphasized.
Exodus from the Golan Plateau
Olmert also expressed an opinion on Israel’s withdrawal from the Golan Plateau, which he believes is an essential precondition for a peace treaty with Syria.
However, this would also require a certain effort from Damascus.
“I don’t recommend a peace deal with Syria solely on the basis of its withdrawal from Golan,” he warned.
“The Syrians are well aware of what they have to relinquish in order to get Golan.
“They must renounce their current ties with Iran; they must give up their relationship with the Hezbollah (Shiite movement); they have to give up the regular support they provide to the terrorism of the Hamas (Shiite movement), al-Qaeda and Jihad (sacramental war) in Iraq,” the prime minister clarified.
