In the background lies the losses of the Depfa subsidiary.
Depfa borrowed for long-distance projects, according to a newspaper report, borrowed money for long-distance projects and refinished itself for a very short maturation – which is now no longer possible due to the lack of confidence in the markets.
In the first half of 2008, the bank’s cash reserves decreased by 95 percent to 2.37 million euros, and the share of equity also declined moderately from 1.6 to 1.45 percent.
Current earnings and net earnings from the treasuries fell to zero compared to the same period of the previous year, with revenues from credit to receivables credits and specific securities, as well as revenues from the liquidation of the provisions of the loan business falling by more than 70 per cent to EUR 5 million.
Only thanks to higher revenues resulting from reimbursing subsequent accounting costs for the restructuring of the former Depfa Group, as well as the reimbursement of interest rates according to the Fee Decree and the shops brought to the companies belonging to the group, there was a relatively modereviated income deficit of approximately 16%.
Following privatization in 1990, the former state-finalist speculating first speculates on corporate real estate loans, which were later segregated at Aeral Bank.
