“Because it’s so well-arranged, I’ve never felt lost, even if Tokyo is the biggest city I’ve ever seen.”
The rats have emotions, too.
He's running in the event of a rat or a moth. Does it mean he's feeling fear?
Scientologist Joseph LeDoux is convinced that animals also have emotions.
How can we see if they feel the same when they behave like us?
Famous Scientists have been publishing their articles for years.
SPIEGEL ONLINE in one of its series spectagates from the answers to "What do you consider to be real without being able to prove?"
I believe that the animals have emotions and other states of consciousness, without me or anyone else being able to prove it. It's true that we can't even prove that other people have consciousness.
In this case, however, we can at least hope that the basic configuration of every human brain is the same.
However, as soon as we look at other races and dissect questions of perception or consciousness in general, we come into a hard-to-walk terrain, because the hardware does not coincide.
“When a rat is in jeopardy, he does the same as many other animals: he pretends to be dead, fleeing or attacking.”
Humans behave in a similar way, suggesting comparable subjective experiences.
I don’t believe that.
The two aspects of the brain structure make it more difficult to transfer our own subjective experiences to animals.
On the one hand, human-associated circuits almost always contain the lateral pre-frontal cortex (because of its role in short-term memory and execution/control functions).
This wide area is more advanced in humans than other vocabulivators, and it seems that other organisms are completely absent.
At least in those aspects of our subsistence that depend on the prefrontal cerebral cortex, e.g. the consciousness of who we are, or it can reasonably be assumed in the case of the ability to design and make a decision that man deviates even from other vocabulivators.
An additional dramatic peculiarity lies in man’s linguistic nature: Since a significant part of the human experience is associated with language, it is often claimed that consciousness depends on it.
In this case the animals can be ruled out.
However, even if it weren’t depending on the language, the language would certainly be influenced by the language, so that the animal consciousness, whatever its kind, is likely to differ strongly from most of our states of consciousness.
It is therefore difficult to find out what the consciousness of the other animals can be.
If the consciousness cannot be measured as an internal and subjective thing, and because of the special hardware it cannot be analyzed on the basis of our own experience, then its research puts us into serious problems.
Most of my decipherments refer to the content of conscious experience, but there is also another aspect of consciousness, which is likely to be more easily approachable to science.
The processes of consciousness of the animals could be investigated, regardless of their content, and this is exactly what happens in the case of experiments on the short-term remembrance of primates, especially in the example of visualization.
However, the hypothesis, examined by Christof Koch and Francis Crick, reliminates the neural relationships of consciousness rather than causal mechanisms: both may be the same or not.
Interestingly, this presumption also emphasizes the importance of the prefrontal cerebral cortex in the creation of visualization.
What happens in terms of emotions?
This is traced back to the fact that an emotional system, such as that of fear, is activated in the brain that is aware of its own activities.
What we call “fear” is the mental state in which we are when the activity of the defensive system localized in the brain (or its consequential, such as bodily reactions), preoccupied a working memory.
In this way, emotions are closely related to areas of the cerebral cortex that are characteristic of primates, especially humans.
Compleated by the linguistic character, the sensing is divided into subtle stages, because with the help of words and grammar we are able to distinguish and categorize states in order to describe them alongside others.
