Prices for airline tickets and hotel rooms will be reduced
In the first half of 2008, European flights and shelters were cheaper than in the previous year.
According to an up-to-date study, flights in Germany became no less than 50 percent cheaper.
The prices of flights and hotel rooms have become more favorable once again – summarised the results of a study by the American Express travel and financial service provider.
The company analysed its booking data in the first half of 2008 and established a significant price decline in the second quarter after the price increase in the first quarter.
Since prices have risen for several years in the hotel market, the accommodations have become cheaper in many places.
Between January and March the prices rose by an average of 6.9 percent in 41 of the 48 European cities surveyed, compared to 2007.
However, in the period from April to June, prices were 3.6 percent lower than in the previous year.
This trend was most clearly seen in Paris and Rome, where the guests had to pay 30-37 percent less than a year earlier.
The American Express sees this in the low exchange rate of the dollar, which has led to “sweakening travel of American tourists”.
Depreciations in hotel room prices are more affecting financial centers such as Frankfurt and London, such as industrial cities such as Hanover, Cologne or Stavanger.
A 26.3 percent increase in hotel prices in the Norwegian city is a European record.
There has also been an increasing change in the prices of airline tickets in the first quarter.
Due to the increase in kerosene, passengers had to pay an average of 7.1 per cent more in the economy class than in 2007, while high-fuel long-distance flights increased by 29.2 per cent.
Between April and June, tourist-class airline ticket prices fell by an average of 12.5 percent compared to the previous quarter, but prices in the first and first division only showed a much more modest change.
In Germany, prices have plummeted even more than in other European countries.
Between January and March, prices increased by 11.9 percent and decreased by 23.6 percent in the second quarter.
According to the study, the prices of domestic flights in Germany were halved.
On the one hand, the reason for the price decrease lies in the declining economic growth, the company says.
Demand has deteriorated in many segments of the flight market.
On the other hand, as a result of the Open Skies agreement liberalising air travel between the United States and Europe, the pressure exerted by the competitive situation is intensifying.
The tense situation is confirmed by the fact that the airlines are canceling their loss-making flights, and a number of airlines filed for bankruptcy in the first half of 2008 – affecting 24 airlines in Europe alone.
