Dark material influences space promenadoids
A mysterious phenomenon has caused perplexity among the researchers for several years. Dark matter affects the velocity of space probes during the so-called swing-by manoeuvre.
Experts fear Albert Einstein’s theory of general relativity should not be completely correct.
The space probe "Ulysses" has flown in outer space for nearly 18 years.
Because of the soon-to-frozen re-frozen fuel lines, he is threatened with frostbite.
Scientists have been in turmoil for many years by a mysterious phenomenon, swing-by an anomaly.
Swing-by is the gravitational rock mast for space probes, during which an auxiliary impetus gains momentum in the gravitational field of a celestial body such as the Earth.
Thus, probes can save a large amount of fuel.
The Cassini-Huygens research probe in Europe, similarly to the Ulysses real-life probe, also used this physical trick.
However, the researchers have astonished for years: Flying objects don’t behave completely the way it would be expected under the ancient gravitational law.
Deficiencies occur in velocity.
The probes unexpectedly accelerating or slowing down.
physicists haven't found an explanation for this effect.
The phenomenon was baptised as a swing-by anomaly.
Some of the experts fear that even Albert Einstein’s theory of general relativity may not be fully correct, concluding the gravitational pull of matter as the curvature of space.
American physicist Stephen Adler at the Princeton Institute for Advanced Study continues to believe that Einstein was not mistaken and is trying to explain swing-by an anomaly with the so-called dark matter discovered by the astronauts in the universe.
“Dark matter may be responsible for the anomaly, which may even be found all the way to the Earth,” Adler writes.
The existence of dark matter was only discovered ten years ago by space explorers.
It obviously exerts a gravitational force that observes its effect on other celestial bodies.
But matter itself is utterly unvisible, i.e. it does not emit or repel any visible light.
Scientists have computed that one-fifth of the total mass of the universe consists of dark matter.
