The price of oil fell sharply due to residual concerns related to the economy
On Monday, oil prices fell sharply due to concerns that U.S. financial market bailout plans would not be able to revitalize the economy.
Crust oil securities fell by seven percent to 99.80 in the morning at the New York Stock Exchange.
Prices have declined by more than USD 20 per barrel since last Monday.
Over the past two weeks, commodities have shattered by Wall Street financial turmoil and some powerful hurricanes in the Gulf of Mexico.
After the prices reached USD 145.29 per barrel in July this month, at the beginning of the month they fell back to almost USD 90 per barrel, before falling again under the influence of the immeasurable instability of the financial markets.
Concerns retaliated on Monday even after congressional executives announced that they had reached an agreement this weekend for an economic rescue operation, which is the largest in history.
Concerns overwhelmed the news that the financial crisis won’t end for a long time.
Citigroup will acquire Wachovia Corporation’s banking business as the latest event of the unfolding crisis, the government announced on Monday.
In the meantime, the governments of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg have partially nationalized European financial conglomerate Fortis.
According to Barclays Capital’s analysts, as a result of the desperitent-of-the-spoken weekend negotiations, “it does not seem to have succeeded in reviving the mood for trading on the stock market.”
As the economic situation deteriorates, demand for commodities, such as oil, is also expected to decline.
“The future of ordinary shares, interest rates, and currency exchange markets around the world has become precarious,” an analyst at Deutsche Bank told investors.
“We don’t think the commodities market will be overlooked by the “epidemic.”
“The biggest question from the commodities market is to the extent to which the “discrimination” infects the whole of the world, in particular with regard to China.
As oil consumption declined due to a slowdown in economic growth, bank analysts cut their expectations for next year, reducing their forecasts for oil and gas prices by 20% for 2009.
