Musulan tomb defamation while Austria shook on the right
Heinz-Christian Strache, from the right-wing Austrian Freedom Party, and Filip Dewinter from the Belgian right-wing Vlaams Belang took part in a demonstration in Antwerp on Thursday, January 17, 2008, in which "Customers Against the Continuation of the Islamic Convergence".
Signs imitating traffic signs depicting mosques crossed with a red-toured mosque.
According to the police, far-right extremists are responsible for deceiving Muslim cemeteries in Austria on the same weekend when the far-right parties made significant progress in national parliamentary elections.
More than 90 tombs have been severely damaged at the Trauni Cemetery near Linz sometime between Friday evening and this morning. According to the police, this is an organized action.
The perpetrators drew Jewish symbols, such as the Star of David, on some tomb, but according to the investigators, they only tried to hide their extrem extremist motives, resulting from hatred of Muslim immigrants.
It’s unclear whether the incident is somehow related to the elections.
A spokesman for the local Islamic community said they were “deeply shaking” when they heard the defamation that occurred shortly before the end of the Muslim religious month of Ramadan.
At the same time, Austria is conducting an examination of conscience following yesterday’s parliamentary elections, during which the language of the political balance has tilted to the right.
The two far-right parties, which won almost 30 percent of the votes, campaigned for the Freedom Party and the Alliance for the Future of Austria, a heavily anti-immigration law, and some of their slogans were denected as xenophobic critics.
Heinz-Christian Strache, the president of the Freedom Party, who won more than 18% of the vote, expressed a campaign against headscarves and shackles and expressed his displeasure with the foods allegedly associated with Islam.
At the party’s final rally in Vienna, he spoke of “European fraternity” to prevent the strengthening of Islam.
Both parties want to ban the construction of mosques, arguing that they are the political symbols of the ongoing Islamization of Austria and Europe.
According to opinion polls and analyses carried out immediately after the far-right’s victory, the change was largely due to a young electoral base who are concerned about their future in the European Union.
