“I don’t take the photographs on the stage, because I would like to present the artist from a personal perspective.” – Günther Bauer Photographer
There's no sets, no ruffles, no dancing shoes.
The grand masters of the flamenco up close, without the excitement of the performance: in order to get to know the everyday face of the dancers.
On the one hand, the artist.
On the other hand, an ordinary person.
And it is not possible to separate these two sides, except in the intimate moments far from the audience, when we can learn a personal movement, we can capture the depths of a captured gaze, or marvel at the beauty of an artist’s idle but expressive hand.
Günther Bauer’s Flamenco-people in his photo series “Greeducation, dancers and guitarists” combines theatricality of singers, dancers and guitarists with this less well-known everyday side of the masters.
Through the photos, flamenco’s unique iconography is presented from different perspectives.
The exhibition, which is part of the Flamenco Photography Month, “reflects the growing international significance of flamenco every day,” he said, adding that Flamenco-people’s “character could be the character of anyone, as at the sight of the pictures, the observer realizes that these pictures do not depict the artists in a typical way.
These people on the streets are artists who are capable of bringing passion, sadness, cheerfulness to the stage and captivate the audience.
At the same time, stage photography is unsuitable for captivating their personality.
Their personalities and characters dwell in the depths of their being.
“I wanted to create an intangible atmosphere that would allow the camera to capture the personalities of flamenco.”
Created by the Cante de las Minas festival, Bauer embarked on a long voyage in 1997, during which he made portraits of flamenco singers, guitarists and dancers.
These Portraits evoke classical photography thanks to the camera’s choice of theme.
The use of various stylistic tools, however, breaks with traditional pictorial representation and suggests modernity. The following thought faithfully reflects Bauer’s idea of Flamno: “It’s a music with traditional roots, but it’s changing from day to day and is always amazing.”
It’s not the dance, it’s not the singing or the music in itself, it’s the artist’s personality and character that enchant the audience.
In recent days, the guiding motif of the works that can be viewed in the El Photomatic Gallery is the search for an encounter with the essence, the artistic razor, which becomes visible through the mode of perception, existence and visualization.
The well-known figures of the flamenco, including Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales, Vicente Amigo are hallmarking this photo collection presenting almost sixty artists.
The project also turned into a flamenco-anthology, from which an album containing the portraits presented at the exhibition will soon be released.
Bauer’s passion for portraits was inspired to reiterate the “Portraits of Veneteran Athlete Athutants” two years ago (“SportARTproject”).
