Recidivists will be obligated to "Alcolock"
The Ministry of the Ministry of the Interior would oblige the driver to install a device that would prevent the start of the vehicle if the driver was intoxicated or did not blow into the device.
The proposal has been put forward in June and is currently being dealt with by the Public Prosecutor’s Office, according to which the pilot project will last for a year.
Since the purpose of the project is to restore traffic, the driver will also have to take part in a recurrence course of transport education.
Apart from the Prosecutor’s Prosecutor’s Office for the Road Safety Prosecutor’s Office (DGT) and the Institute of Motor Safety Technology Foundation (FITSA).
According to statistics, 30-75 percent of motorists who have been arrested several times for drunk driving are in the car again.
The Alcolock would prevent this, because the system connected to the vehicle determines the alcohol level of the blown-blled air. If it is greater than the permissible, or if the driver does not blow into the device at all, the system does not allow the car to be started.
The cost of more than 1000 euros would be borne by the violator.
A 2007 U.S. study has shown that vehicles equipped with such instruments can reduce the number of offences up to 65% in the first year of installation.
It is estimated that 115 people’s lives could be saved every a year in Spain.
The measure focuses on violations for two reasons: on the one hand, it makes it easier to prevent them from becoming a criminal offense.
On the other hand, it is easier to implement it, since the implementation of the legal reform is carried out in the usual way, while the necessary criminal reform would require an amendment to the Fundamental Law.
The instrument has not yet been commercially available on the Spanish market, although it has already been installed in some industrial and public service vehicles in experimental mode.
The Chamber of Deputies will vote tomorrow on an initiative by the Spanish Socialist Workers’ Party, which calls for the integration of the system into school buses as a French model.
