The blood is safe again.
Hema-Québec celebrated the tenth anniversary of its foundation yesterday. President Dr. Francine Décary had a good reason to celebrate, because Héma-Québec has become one of the safest and most effective blood donation companies in the world.
Most importantly, he succeeded in regaining people’s trust, which seriously shattered by the Incredible blood scandal in the 1980s and 1990s.
As it is well known, a national blood reserve treated by the blood-supply service of the Red Cross in Canada was infected with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (HIVs) (HIV) and hepatitis C viruses in the 1980s.
Following the drama and the general upheaval, the Government of Canada launched an official investigation under the leadership of Judge Horace Krever in February 1994 to find out the circumstances leading to the tragedy and to formulate proposals to avoid similar cases in the future.
Whilst Judge Krever delayed the submission of his report, some provinces set up their own committee aimed at designing a new blood management system for Canada.
The Gélineau committee held this task in Quebec.
The Gélineau and Krever reports resulted in the creation of two blood-providing services in Canada: Héma-Québec, serving Quebec, and all other provinces and the Canadian Blood Transminoential Service Company responsible.
“When Héma-Québec started its activity in 1998, only 38% of people had confidence in Québec’s blood-supply system,” Décary recalls.
The situation was deplorable.
“In the following years, however, as a result of improving the safety of blood products and our work in the field, we succeeded in regaining people’s trust.” Décary proudly states that at the beginning of 2008, almost 94% of the population of Quebec considered Héma-Québece to be trustworthy.
However, Dr. Décary doesn’t forget “confidence is an extremely frightening thing.”
Since its foundation on September 28, 1998, Héma-Québec has applied the principle of prudence.
During this period, a number of cases have been discovered in Europe when a new variant of mad cow disease, i.e. Creutzfeldt-Jakob, has also infected people, according to the doctor, who has ruled the company since its founding in 1998.
“We didn’t even know if this disease was transmitted by blood.
For this reason, we relied on one of the most important recommendations of the Krever and Gélineau reports. Accordingly, if we are unsactivated and there is a risk of developing a serious disease, we need to apply exclusion measures. In this case, Creutzfeldt-Jakob was a disease that causes brain lesions, inevitably lead to death.
Therefore, in April 1999, we did not accept don't don't don't take blood from people who have spent more than a month in the United Kingdom since 1980.
A few years ago, it was established beyond any doubt that the disease was spread by blood.
This case convinced us that it made sense to apply the principle of prudence.”
In 1999, a technique appeared on the market, which allows for a more susceptible diagnosis of the HIV virus. This nucleic acid test (NAT), which in the blood of blood donors is able to directly detect the virus itself, rather than anti-vironmental anti-virondled antibodies, on which the previously used tests were built.
With the help of the NAT test, we were able to short-speak to the window, which lasts from infestation until the virus can be detected in the blood with the help of our technical devices, the specialist explains.
This period could be lowered for a period of less than a week.
Thus, they significantly reduced the risk that blood donors were unknowingly unknowingly inflicting the blood supply, as well as not being detected.
These developments have greatly reduced the risk of the spread of AIDS through blood transfusions.
“Today, there’s a risk that someone will get HIV-infected-infected blood is 12 million for one.
“In other words, it means we should allocate 12 million blood products while someone is infected with HIV.
“Because Héma-Québec distributes 400,000 to 500,000 to 500,000 blood products each year, it would take more than 24 years for such a case to become infected with HIV through a blood product.
“I don’t think anyone can now accept or prescribe blood transfusions in order to restore their health,” says Héma-Québet’s chief executive.
“The challenge for the next decade will be to maintain the reliability of our products.
We'll never know what kind of new pathogen enters the system.
It wasn’t until the last year that the chikungunya virus appeared on the island of Réunion, which, like the Western Nile virus, gets into the blood with insect bites.
That’s why we have to be on our guard all the time,” Dr. Décary continued, stressing that in addition to the emergency reserves, Héma-Québec had to take care of sufficient permanent reserves.
“Actinuation of the population also increases.
Since anti-cancer treatments have become more aggressive, patients often need replacement of red blood cells or platelets.
The ageing of the population also has an effect on the population of blood donations, as the number of people in the network for donating blood makes up only 3% of the Quebec population.
However, every healthy person, at least 18 years of age, fulfilling the acquaintance conditions can donate blood every 56 days, i.e. six times a year.
On an average, 14% of the applicants do not accept blood because, for example, they are infected with AIDS or malaria. Those who have been in a country where malaria thieves thieves are not allowed to donate blood for a year.
“We have 3% of our reserves to meet the needs of hospitals.
It's very rare that we're running out of supplies.
On the other hand, we have to increase the number of blood donors to the extent that the existing ones get older, because after a while they can no longer don’t don’t don’t don’t don’t don’t don’t donate.
But it’s very difficult to convince people to donate blood, but it’s even more difficult to keep and get them to become regular blood donors.
“In this fast-moving world in today’s fast-moving world, there’s hardly a single hour to get blood,” Francine Décary said.
Based on an annual budget of USD 297 million, Héma-Québec provides not only blood products but also human tissue, such as bone transplants, heart valves, eyeball tissues and skin tissue, which is used almost exclusively for severely-burned individuals.
These tissues will be analyzed, transformed and frozen, and then placed at the Hema-Québec, which also manages the only public stem cell bank in Quebec.
The umbilical cord, which was once treated only as a medical waste, can be extracted with blood that is rich in stem cells, so it also makes it possible to treat serious diseases (such as leukemia).
“It’s been a few weeks since we’ve been able to celebrate the thousand umbilical cord-blood-patterned patterns in our bank, and we plan to remove more than a thousand new samples every year.
“At the beginning of next year, we’ll probably start offering them for organ transplants,” said Dr. Décary, according to whom the umbilical cord blood removal program is very popular nowadays.
“A lot of mothers are sad that they don’t have the opportunity to do so, really, because Héma-Québec is incapable of making this complex and cost-intensive program available in all hospitals in Quebec.
