Barrick Gold Orwellian
Le Devoir published a letter from Patrick J. Garver, vice-president and CEO of the Barrick Gold mine in Canada on September 17.
Following Orwellian's exposition, Garver states that the world’s largest gold-producing company has filed a six-million-dollar defamation lawsuit against three authors and a small-money publishing company in order to initiate an so-so-called “public debate”.
Even though the judges are independent and unprofessional, their decision, especially in defamation lawsuits, is made not on the basis of the truth, but on the basis of the law.
Unsurprisingly, Garver was deeply silent about this fundamental difference.
For Barrick Gold, the best way to controversy in a public debate would be to destroy anyone whose stance is contrary to him?
Irrespective of the outcome of the lawsuit, the authors and publishers of the book Noir Canada will have to spend significant sums of money on defense and combat in an unequal struggle, where on the other hand there is an army of lawyers backed by almost inexhaustible resources of the mining industry.
Such unequal struggle, by the way, often uses the tactic of destroying the defendant and making a friendly agreement that not only silences the opposite, but even intimidates those who would like to re-negotiate.
“If Barrick Gold really wanted a public debate, he should have expressed his views in front of the general public.”
Although in many other countries where Barrick Gold is active, this happens in a different way, in Canada the disputing parties don’t hit each other with a stick, they say their thoughts.
Therefore, it would have been sufficated for the mine to open its books and dossiers and to prepare for a real debate in the press, media and publications, paying for independent research groups that might have shed light on the issue.
But Barrick Gold doesn’t seem to trust the general public, and a single gesture will sweep aside the petitions and letters written in defense of the authors of Noir Canada.
Perhaps Mr Garver could explain how he can initiate a "public" debate if at the same time he disregards the opinion of this public?
For the vice-president's mind, this word is likely to just mean bridging lawyers, not Canada's all-in-a-dwelling population: the meaning of the term thus narrows.
In a democratic state, even defamators others are entitled to freedom of speech.
Barrick Gold doesn’t understand—or doesn’t want to understand—whetsemble—whether Noir Canada’s authors and publishers are mostly supported by the public because it condemns the mine’s rudimentary procedure.
The public is ready to listen to Barrick and make a verdict, but based on civilized arguments, not by a lawsuit aimed at scrutinising for many. (In connection with the term "public debate," Barrick Gold recently called on the authors of Noir Canada to refrain from using this term.)
In addition to Garver’s surreal exhilaments, Barrick Gold’s process poses an unextended attack on the freedom of university research and the search for the truth, which are the building blocks of any democratic society.
On the whole, the procedure denies the quotition of credible sources and the right to a debate on the facts and their interpretation, which form the basis of intellectual activities.
If a country such as Canada can only study and discuss topics under the burden of litigation that you don’t like the rich companies of today’s world, then who will take the courage to do that?
If Barrick Gold really wanted an open and transparent debate, it could do so according to the scientific standards applied by the authors of Noir Canada.
You have your right to respond.
For a very long time we have been able to deal with controversies, disagreements and conflicts.
Barrick Gold’s ill-considered procedure clearly illustrates that, contrary to its claims, it did not want to have an open controversy.
This represents a loss for both the world of universities and the institution of public debate in Canada. It’s time for governments to finally put in place measures to restrict legal proceedings for such abuse.
Wall Street Ambulance: Democrats and Republicans Have Acquired
“Revolutious and Democrat executives of the Bush administration and the U.S. Congress have finally reached an agreement yesterday on an economic bailout that is the biggest plan since the Great Depression of the 1930s.
The 700-billion-dollar U.S.-dollar-dollar-dollar-dollar plan aimed at curbing the financial crisis that has been raging on Wall Street for months will still have to be approved by the two Houses of Congress.
Substigential points of the plan: more effective control of the implementation by the MPs; new mechanisms to ensure the repayment of public funds provided; new provisions to maintain the number of houses seized due to non-payment; the right of access to the salaries of the managers of companies benefiting from the salaries.
U.S. Treasury U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, Nancy Pelosi, President of the U.S. House of Compute Judd Gregg and Republican MP Judd Gregg announced the good news yesterday, a few minutes before midnight after the two-day marathon trial came to an end.
“We’ve made a big step forward towards a solution that will work effectively in the markets,” said Henry Paulson of the Bush administration.
“Everything we’ve done with the goal of saving the real economy and U.S. citizens from the current crisis on Wall Street,” he said.
“The most important aspect is that if we don’t do that, there’s going to be a huge amount of tackling, chaos and confusion in America’s everyday lives,” said Republican Congressman Judd Gregg.
Negotiations were accelerated in the last few hours, fearing another failure would have a detrimental effect on investor sentiment.
The agreement was given a deadline until the opening of the stock exchange this morning.
The two presidential nominees, who had joined the negotiations late, seemed willing to reach an agreement; however, they were not happy that the situation had escalated to the end.
Democrcipant President Barack Obama has attributed the expansion of the agreement with elements, such as provisions relating to the preservation of home ownership or the salaries of executives of corporations assisted.
“When we ask taxpayers to take some extraordinary action for the irresponsibility of other people, it’s not very welcome.
“These steps are necessary,” he reiterated on an American television show.
“When his opponent, John McCain, was asked by another channel if he would vote in favour of the proposed plan, he said, “I would like to know the details, but I don’t.”
“What I’ve read broadly, you can already see that it’s about something that we all need to digest and through which we can move on.
“It’s an opportunity not to do anything, it’s simply unacceptable,” he said.
According to the 700-billion-dollar plan, they would use taxpayers’ money to buy the dubious demands of U.S. financial institutions, some risky, unsaleable assets.
The plan was preceded by a 14-month crisis triggered by real estate bubble burst. This first led to the collapse of risky real-equivalent real-equivalent real estate (secondary mortgage loans), then spread to other debt (commercial) securities, and ultimately caused the freezing of liquid resources and the booming of banks and financial institutions.
The first draft agreement was overthrown last week by a group of Republican deputies.
Looks like we've recovered from this dead-sculded street since the democrats agreed that certain "poisoning" assets of companies in difficulty would only be insured rather than acquiring by the government.
In order to ensure that all members of the Congress are satisfied, the plan also includes a provision that stipulates that if the government has to bear the losses under the program for five years, it may levy new taxes in order to recover its resources.
As part of the new plan, a tranche of USD 250-billion-dollar would first be dismantled, followed by an additional USD 100 billion if the president deems it necessary.
The disbursement of the remaining USD 350-billion-dollar would have to be approved by Congress with a further vote.
In exchange for the assistance, Washington acquires a stake in subsidized companies, through which he receives dividends from the profits, and which he can sell after the end of the crisis.
The federal government will have the opportunity to delay the sequestration of real estate as collateral for the mortgages covered by the plan. In addition, it can set the maximum value of wages and severance payments to the managers of the companies affected by the crisis.
The creators of the plan would like the plan to be approved by the House of Deputies today and by the Senate on Wednesday.
However, in spite of their efforts, it is still not certain that the plan will be approved.
“Yesterday there have reiterated allegations against the plan among both Republicans and democrats.
“It’s very clear that Washington doesn’t hear your voice,” said Republican Scott Garrett, a member of the house’s finance committee.
“Yes, we have serious financial problems.
Yes, there are solutions.
“There’s nothing that would benefit taxpayers.”
Brad Sherman, a Democrat who heads the small congressional group called the skeptic “skeptical faction,” describes things in a different way, but comes to the same conclusion.
“Furties of excellent economic experts, including three Nobel laureate scientists, have written to us to ask Congress not to hurry and carefully examine which path they choose,” Sherman said.
At the same time, it has been reconvened in Europe that this is a global crisis.
Following a crisis meeting, the three Benelux states announced that they nationalized a part of the Fortis banking group worth 11.2 billion euros (USD 16.3 billion).
Interference between the governments of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg became necessary after the French BNP Paribas announced that it would not acquire its competitor on the verge of bankruptcy, after it lost 2 million euros (USD 2.9 billion) as a result of the U.S. substantiated sub-mortar mortgage crisis.
With 85,000, Fortis is one of the 20 largest European banks and the ten largest insurers.
“It’s not a waste of money,” Dutch finance minister Wouter Bos said yesterday.
“We are gaining voting rights and influence at the bank.
“It’s exactly something that depositors and households appreciate now that the situation promises to be precarious.”
On the other side of La Manche, the British government seems to be planning to nationalize another bank that has been swept away by the financial crisis.
According to the British press, the government would take over the list of Bradford and Bringley’s ongoing loans, of which real estate locomotives amounted to 41 billion pounds (75.5 billion US dollars).
Spain’s Santander bank has announced yesterday that they will acquire the British bank’s subsidiaries and customers.
Bradford and Bringley would be the second-largest bank that the British government intends to nationalize since the beginning of the crisis.
(1925-2008) – Farewell to the Actor Ball
According to some, the eyes of beautiful Azure blue are his main trump card, others say he was his main weakness.
Has an American acquaintance actor ever got rid of his narcastic mirror?
Yeah, I don't, and I don't.
Due to his shrewness, sensitivities and ingenital talent, he was also far removed from the cherish-education of the true great monstrosity of the art world.
But what a huge trajectory!
Paul Newman died of lung cancer at the age of '83 on Friday in his Connecticut estate.
There was a less monumental personality than Brando, whom he resembled, and who for a long period of time was a competitor on the screen for a long period of time. He was more restrained and unequivocally more vulnerable in his roles, but he also promoted freedom and demolition demolition.
Mixing the blood often comes with extraordinary children.
Newman was born in 1925 in Okio to a German Jewish father and a mother of Hungarian descent.
The European roots contributed to the birth of a symbolic figure of the American dream.
He didn’t think too much about himself, which only increased his merits.
It embodied the ideal of the "self-made man" and the idea of a warrior who had been broken in the Pacific War. In the early 1950s, he studied drama at Yale University's drama, then at Actor's Studio in New York before he exploded into the world of theatre, television, and shortly afterwards.
In addition to being at the forefront of civil rights, as a motorcyclist as a motorcyclist he won second place in the 24 Hours of Le Mans in 1979. However, he was considered a sex symbol, even though he had been happily marrigeled with actress Joanne Woodward since 1958.
We can no longer find out what the downsides of this mysterious aloof, acquitted an actor, dedicated to his work who hated the glitter of the show-to-business. This will remain a mystery forever.
He was a sincere man, in the noblest sense of the word.
The death of Paul Newman at the end of the week shocked the movie fans: in his big roles we can only be seen on the black screen of memories.
After reviewing his oeuvre, the 7th U.S. art came before us, the cinema has been dotted with continuous changes for more than fifty years.
