"Rachel (adapted by the Canadian author Margaret Laurence's novel) starred a fine portrait of a melancholy woman starring Joanne Woodward.
"Harry and his son," made in 1984, commemorates his son, Scott, who died in a drug overdose.
Yesterday Newman was commemorated by countless spectators. His close-knit and long-awaited companion, Robert Redford (with whom they starred together "Threat Troubles" and "Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid), said: "Your career has made my life and the world better.
Engenuity and the joy of being: this was the secret of this great actor’s vibrating personality we have now lost.
In addition to his legendary career, he is obscured by his struggle for civil rights, the environment, for independent film production, and his remarks criticizing the debauchery of Hollywood.
We’ve been able to see his picture on the packaging of ditches, sauces, biscuits and other foods. He did it with a bad taste, although at the beginning he thought it was funny.
Revenue from such sales, however, has always been redistributed to a foundation set up for children with the disease.
Newman never sat on his laurels: he was always committed to the profession.
In a letter left to five of his daughters, he wrote: “Your father has always been unimaginably thankful for the star of fortune.
“It’s been a privilege for me to be here.”
It’s also a privilege for us to accompany the career of such a brilliant and generous American artist.
He's a citizen, above all else.
What did they produce at Zuschlag & Tsai’s account factory?
Soon, on October 8, the trial of the criminal proceedings against János Zuschlag and his associates will begin.
The politician and his comrades are accustomed to fraud and other criminal offences, such as falsification of documents, causing particularly significant damage in a criminal organisation.
According to the Prosecutor’s Office, the received tender funds were used for their own purposes, and the fulfillment of the tasks was often verified by untrue invoices.
State and self-government funds (approximately 65 million forints total) migrated partly into the cash register of the Young Left and partly into the pockets of certain persons.
The method, which was also presented by the investigative authority, was that the tender funds won were collected on the basis of cost invoices, behind which there was no real fulfillment, and the associations had never complied with the agreed obligation.
The indictment said most of the bills were fake.
The invoice blocks were purchased in the Józsefváros market, and the circular stamps were produced in Kiskunhalas.
The name of which of the companies the production of invoices was decided, among other things, it was decided on a boulevard in Balaton-felvidék, where János Zuschlag dismissed István Lados.
According to the testimony of the latter, it had to look at pensions and restaurants.
He asked for an invoice for his/her consumption there, after which the invoices to be filed for the tenders of the Ministry of Youth and Sport, the Ministry of Social Affairs and Labour and the Municipality of Budapest were drawn up.
This was mostly recorded by István Lados with his partner, András Őri, who, according to our information, he mostly wrote the reports necessary to win the subsidies.
One of Lados' "study trips" was a beer house.
According to the two invoices issued by the Zuschlags dated in 2003, the catering establishment ensured the meals of the ninety-multi-mundant group of the Secure Pont Association.
However, according to the company’s manager, they wouldn’t be able to get into the restaurant at the same time.
In the same way, an invoice issued in the summer of 2002 to the Foundation for a Better Future (Revolution for the Third Millennium) was teemed with formal and substantive defects.
There was only a three-bedroom pension in Badacsonytomaj, more than 10 people couldn’t fall asleep one night, but the bill was about one hundred and thirty guests.
But the imaginative form-fillers also exaggerated when Pigal Kft., which also deals with vortices, was designated, in whose accounts – according to the information given to the prosecution – did not even contain documents for the Better Future and Hoppá.
Győző Farkas, the head of the company, told hvg.hu that when he was summoned at the prosecutor’s office and invoices were presented, he immediately knew that they were false documents.
“The stamp had the wrong name of our business and our tax number.”
He added that in addition to the formal reasons, it was also untrue that according to the invoice a hundred people were accommodated, because they have a total of eleven rooms, in which there are not so many places.
In addition, the invoice was for catering, while their restaurant hadn’t been in operation for a long time.
Grabbing billmakers did not even look into the fact that the Budapest travel agency they are looking is only organising trips from abroad, so it stumblingly marked travel in Hungary as a service on the cash payment account, which also did not coincide with the company’s account-issuing practices.
In addition, as it turned out in the course of the investigation, VAT was also included on the document, even though travel activities to countries outside the European Union were VAT-free.
Among other things, the amount of the VAT was also a mistake on the four documents issued by Hotel Révfülöp Kft. for a Better Future, as the company’s service is not subjected to 25 per cent, but 15 per cent, and the hotel’s employees only issued a machine invoice, but the invoice produced was manual.
In addition, “exhibitors” broke an account by the fact that in the case of a single company, road freight transport was designated as an activity, according to the prosecutor’s procedure, which was not carried out by the company originally based on investment consultancy.
In addition, due to formal discrepancies – an inappropriate invoice format, not a real stamp – it has also been ouded that the bills for Together for the Third Millennium, the 21st-Court Cultural Association and the Biztos Point were fake.
Just like the Ravenna Bt. or the Kunfehértó Sports Camp, whose deputies denied that the invoices were issued by them.
The counterfeiters also did not pay attention, for example, to the fact that Nyíregyháza HIB Kft. was in liquidation (2001) at a time when the group of the Association for the Young City was eating there, therefore the liquidation commissioner of the company – as far as we know – even denied that he would have issued the paper.
Consciously issued
Another group of account issuers are made up of those who were aware that they had to issue a fictitious invoice.
At the request of the Young Left-You-You-You-You-You-Socialists, the Foundation for Opportunity for the Third Millennium Foundation issued an invoice for about four million forints.
According to the indictment, one of the defendants Ludovic Györfi persuaded the head of Collab Ltd. to issue the fictitious invoice.
He also cooperated with the Zuschlags by Biogate Ltd., which is currently in liquidation, which in 2003 provided the Foundation for a Better Future, the Atlasz Association and the Vörösfenyő Association with invoices issued for roughly five million forints, behind which there was no real economic activity.
There is no movement of money either, because the invoices were refurbished afterwards, but the organizations already forgot to include this in their documents.
The next group of the companies issuing the invoice belong to those that may be linked to the defendants.
Accordingly, Iván Kubatov was the delivery agent of the Ukrainian managing director of Delta Trió Kft., which deals with fuel and vehicle trade in Budapest.
The company issued a number of invoices in October, November, and December 2005 for the sale of computing devices worth approximately HUF 18 million.
According to the datas of the investigation, these invoices were purchased by Kiskunhalas Cash Investment Kft., which has been in liquidation since September 14, 2005.
The managing director of this company was a Romanian man, to whom Iván Kubatov would have forwarded the documents related to the company between 2002 and 2005.
According to Kubatov in the course of the investigation, Kubatov acknowledged that he issued an invoice on behalf of the Ltd. to the Atlas, the Hoppa and the Together for the Third Millennium Foundation, and that he had received their consideration.
At the request of Iván Kubatov, Romanian mascuals founded Fa-Bani Jos Faipari Kft. and Jos-Investment Bt.
In fact, however, none of the companies actually worked, which, according according to the defendant, was due to the fact that they did not get a job.
Nevertheless, the companies were benefited, because at the request of Zsolt Szente, a colleague of the defendant, lawyer Zsolt Szente, who also made the papers of company foundations, issued invoices on behalf of the associations related to Zuschlag: Atlas and Y-Generation.
Kubatov gave a total of 15 invoices on behalf of Cash Investment, the Delta Trio, Fa-Bani Jos, Jos Investment and Linaxtrade, worth HUF 14.2 million.
The prosecutor’s office believed that 10 per cent of the amounts indicated in the invoices and the VAT hit Kubatov’s hands.
The man even used Szi-Beri Bt. to issue fictitious invoices, which were produced for the competition of the Secure Point.
Y-Generation and the Alliance for Modern Hungary filed in January 2006 an application for the Táncsics Mihály Foundation (TMA), a public benefit organization of the socialist party.
They also won 500-500 thousand forints, but the amounts were not used for the purposes indicated in the tender.
In order not to find out, the five fictitious invoices received from Works Team Kft. were filed with TMA.
By the way, these invoices were found during a search of Árpád Kemecsei’s office at the Ministry of Education.
At the trial, some of the defendants, as he did at the Prosecutor’s Office, is likely to report on the process of account production.
It may be most interesting if the first accused himself, János Zuschlag, decides to unveil everything.
His testimony so far isn’t very detailed, and even though he has repeatedly pledged to speak comprehensively, he hasn’t done it yet.
The question is whether the socialists made a deal with him in exchange for reimbursing a large part of the desperation of fifty-million forints, and Zusi will take the blame.
By the way, he could have been offended by today, as his requests to alleviate the circumstances of his detention, i.e. to place him under house arrest were constantly on declining ears.
“While the perpetrators of economic crimes that cause much greater damage and remunerate it are free to walk, or at least wait in their own homes for the end of the proceedings, they do not have the opportunity to do so”.
Psychology of Overprotacy: Why do we spend more money?
At the auctions, buyers tend to spend more money and over-evaluate commodities.
This well-known and seemingly irrational behavior has uncovered the neurological-economominomic foundations of neuroscientology and economic researchers at the University of New York.
According to a study published in the journal Science, “People don’t want to fall behind in a type of competition, such as eBay’s auction, so they pay too high a price.”
The research attempted to map the neurological schema of this "match" with the help of experimental auctions.
One group of the volunteers competed the lottery, and the other group took part in a player auction, while using the functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) method, they examined the active brain regions of experimental individuals.
Participants could win money in both games, but during the auction game – in which the overbiding of the other party is an important element – the striatum, under the cerebral cortex, activated intensively during the loss of the game.
In addition to several other functions, this area of the cerebral brain plays a role in the "self-relief" mechanism of the nervous system.
Therefore, according to the researchers, the tide of the price can be explained by avoiding the violent reaction of striatum.
In subsidizing their theoretical, the economists of the research group also carried out a financial follow-up study.
There are three groups bidding against a partner under different conditions.
The control group was simply communicated to what the real value of the given product was, and they made their bets.
In addition, another group was pledged that if they came out of the game victorious, they would be rewarded to get another 15 dollars in money.
The third group, on the other hand, received just 15 dollars before the auction, but the members of the group also lost this money in the case of a losing auction.
Therefore, the difference between the two groups only emphasized the win for one of them and the loss for the other.
However, proving the neuroscientists’ hypothetical hypothetical hypotheses, the members of the team playing against “situation” were willing to bet even higher than the “suffitive” groups.
“These results cannot be deducied from any pre-existing economic theory.
So far, with the joy of victory and risk aversion, they have only explained the phenomena at the auction.
“It’s just the neurological imaging methods that helped resolve the discrepancy between these two theories with a third explanation,” said Andrew Schotter, a professor of pharmaceutical faculties at the University of New York.
Hepatitis: when should we see a doctor?
Approximately half a billion people in the world suffer from chronic hepatitis B or C infection, and the leading cause of death in Hungary mentions chronic liver disease.
There are 100,000 people infected with hepatitis in Hungary, but there are only 7,000 people, which is the cause of which: patients often don’t know that they are infected.
Hepatitis is the collective name for infectious liver prerogations caused by various viruses.
There is a lot of similarities in their symptoms of hepatitis and jaundice, but their pathogens and the diseases caused differ from each other.
The hepatitis viruses spread in two main forms, depending on the pathogen: Hepatitis A and E through the gastrointestinal gastrointestinal tract (e.g. infected food, water), hepatitis B, C, D, and other viruses with blood and body fluids (obasset, vaginal discharge, saliva).
It is adviscitable for travellers to protect themselves against the A and B viruses by vaccination, there is no vaccine against C.
Symptoms of Hepatitis A occur two to three weeks after consuming the infected food, generally accompanied by malaise, weakness, abdomen, nausea.
Subsequently, jaundice can be seen on the skin and the white of the eyes, the stool is bright, the urine becomes dark, the healing can last for two to three weeks.
A million people around the world are dying of a more severe type of disease, liver cancer caused by hepatitis B and cirrhosis per cirrhosis.
The main reason for this is that it is estimated that only one in ten infected patients receive medication.
Hepatitis C can also have very serious consequences: if it is untreated, it can also lead to cirrhosis and liver cancer.
Most of the infected are unaware of their disease, because the pathogen devadicates the cells of the liver, in many cases the first symptoms occur only 20 to 30 years.
Therefore, it is very important to undergo early prevention and regular screening tests.
There is far more such illness in three groups than the average: people who have undergone blood transfusions before 1991, between drug addicts and tattooists.
There are currently 200 million people infected with the chronic hepatitis C virus in the world.
Since the disease can already be detected by a routine laboratory examination, it is advisable to go to the general practitioner when detecting any symptoms of unfamiliar origin.
Recognising the disease can be cured in time, the virus can be eradicated from the body, thus eliminating the infertility and preventing the development of cancer.
September 30 is World Hepatitis Day, held for the first time in 2004 by organizations representing patients around the world for the first time.
Its aim is to draw attention to chronic hepatitis caused by viruses, the causes of its development and the importance of early detection of the disease.
Does anyone who believe in God hurts any less?
According to a recent study, the religious faith acts as a kind of painkiller, which may have facilitated the death of Catholic martyrs.
Scientists at the University of the University of Oxford carried out an interesting experiment, examining how faith in God influences people’s sense of pain, the British Telegraph reported.
As part of the rather bizarre study, 12 Catholics and the same atheists were given electroshock while they all studied a painting depicting the Virgin Mary.
According to the results, the Catholic believers were able to rule out a large part of the pain.
Thanks to the bleaking technique of the human brain, it also turned out that the Catholics were able to activate the part of their brains responsible for regulating pain.
The study is the first in a series of studies involving scientists, philosophers and theologians from different parts of the university.
The study proceeded precisely by attaching a shining device to the left hand of the subject, which supplied the electroshock.
Scientists in the experiment asked participants to look hard at two paintings, the Virgin Mary painted by Sassoferrato in the 17th-century, and Leonardo da Vinci's "Bloody with Hermelin in the 15th century".
The creators of the investigation hoped that the sight of the Virgin Mary would create a kind of religious condition among the believers, while the da Vinci image was chosen because it was not too different from the other, and they thought to be a sedative.
Experimental subjects spent half an hour in an MRI scanner, during which time they received 20 electroshocks in four consecutive episodes while either viewing the religious-themed or the general picture.
The Catholics reported that during the sight of the Virgin Mary, they felt "security" as if they were "taken careful" and "retired, filled them with peace".
While viewing the religious-themed image, 12 percent less pain was reported than when Leonardo’s creation was seen in front of them.
Psychologist Miguel Farias, who himself participated in the study, believes that a similar effect can be achieved with any image with a strong effect on athetians as well, only the right photo has to be found.
“We need to find a picture for whom they feel very positive, like a mother or a father,” he said.
Anglican Bishop Tom Wright greeted the results of the study.
“Profesitment of the faith should and, in many cases, changes our personalities,” Wright added.
After the great results, the only question is, if Leonardo or a painter with a similar caliber show an image of one of the madonans to the believers, how would it affect their pain control?
Is the sight of the quality of the Virgin Mary even better?
