The Warsaw Stock Exchange made an offer to buy the Prague Stock Exchange
Two-hundred-million-euro – about five billion Czech korunas – was donated by the Warsaw Stock Exchange to buy the 100 percent share package of the Prague Stock Exchange.
Ludwik Sobolewski, the president of the Warsaw Stock Exchange, reaffirmed the offer, which was made by the Poles, even though the Warsaw Stock Exchange had previously been disqualified among the possible new owners of the Prague Stock Exchange.
The reason for this is that the Warsaw Stock Exchange is in the hands of the state.
Ninety-five per cent of the shares are in the hands of the Polish state, which intends to sell more than 60 per cent of the shares within a few months.
The Czech economic daily E15 has a new knowledge that the offer of the Poles represents the highest amount, which has so far been donated by those interested for the Prague Stock Exchange.
According to the Czech newspapers, the Vienna Stock Exchange, the Nasdaq Transatlantic Stock Exchange (New York Stock Exchange), the London Stock Exchange and the Deutsche Börse have submitted an offer so far.
Petr Koblic, the head of the Prague Stock Exchange, said last week that the Warsaw Stock Exchange would be the Worst new owner among those interested, and he did not rule out that in the future it could lead to the disappearance of the Prague Stock Exchange.
The value of the Prague Stock Exchange is valued at 5 to 10 billion kroner by market experts.
According to the agreement of the shareholders, about 90 per cent of the securities could be sold.
The largest shareholder of the Prague Stock Exchange is Patria Finance’s investment bank with an approximately 25 percent stake, followed by Ceská Sporitelna bank 14.7 percent and Tiger Holding’s Four S.
