Immit the flying of the bumblebee by an intelligent car
What would be the best way to develop technologies to avoid collision if it wasn’t imitating obstacle detectors in the natural world?
The bees, for example.
In the surprising project presented at CEATEC in Japan, bumblebees gave Nissan’s engineers a strategic idea to build the next generation of collision-avoiding systems.
In cooperation with the University of Tokyo, Nissan has built the BR23C bio-Imitation Autorobot control system, a robotized mini-car that has been endowed with bee properties, with the ultimate goal of promoting the development of a system that eliminates all collisions.
Every bee develops an oval-shaped personal space during flight, which in fact is very similar to our safety shield, said Kazuhhiro Doi, Chief Technology PR and R&D at the exhibition.
An important role in the combined eyes of the uterus is important, which can be seen in every angle of more than 300-destroke.
To cope with its complex eye function, the engineers resorted to the idea of a laser telescope.
Before the BR23C in a 180-degree field of view, it identifies the obstacles within a distance of 2 meters, calculates their distance and sends a signal to the on-board micro-processor, which is immediately transformed into an operation to avoid a collision.
During a fraction of a second, the obstruction is detected, then the car robot, imitating the movements of the uterus, immediately changes the direction by turning its wheels necessary to avoid a collision.
In a different way from any current system, this evasive manoeuvre is carried out in a completely instinctive manner.
“If this wasn’t the case, the car robot wouldn’t be able to react quickly enough to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshiyuki Andoh, head of the Mobility Laboratory and the car-robot project.
Responsibly and promptly, as this technology corresponds to the most vulnerable and insatisfactory layer of the safety shield, a layer within which a collision is currently considered unavoidable.
The whole process should reflect what a uterus does in order to evacuate other bees.
This process has to be carried out in a blink of an eye.
However, in a way other than the bees, the discrepancy should not be directed upwards or diagonally, it can only be two dimensional, and only in the direction of the swirability of the wheels.
In this way, instead of an infinite opportunity to evacuate other uteruses for bees, only rotating movements and accelerating and decelerating have been used as an option for the car robot.
The BR23C uses lithium-polymer batteries that are lighter and have a higher energy density, which means they have a longer service life than the lithium ion version.
According to Andoh, an instinctively intelligent reaction is important, it is not the ability to process or store the data.
This device has to process and react only a few input signals per second.
There’s absolutely no need to preserve them and react with an intuitive logic based on data from previous maneuvers, so it uses only a few dozen kilobytes of memory.
You don’t need gigabytes of or megabyte-sized memories.
“It’s the first step in developing an Immediate Disagreement technology that Nissan estimates it will be able to enter mass-manufactured cars within a period of ten years.”
An additional application can be the integration of the unit with the GPS satellite navigation system to lead the driver to the specified end destination by avoiding the obstacles on the road.
