A written version has been drafted of several instalments of an economic bailout package law of USD 700 billion aimed at the purchase of banks’ bad loans.
In the first round, they spend half of the money.
Congressional leaders have come to an end to a debate over a total of 700 billion dollars in a week-long week-long debate, Reuters reported.
“We have made significant progress,” said Nancy Pelosi, Democrat president of the U.S. Chamber of Deputies.
Both presidential nominees, Barack Obama and John McCain, also supported the compromise on the package.
Treasury Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson said that the program will work and will be effective, but there will also be a need for further action. Pelosi says this is not about rescuing Wall Street, but about reversing the chariot of the economy.
The written version of the package was made public in front of the opening of the Asian markets on Sunday at 8 p.m. (i.e. at 2:00 a.m. on CET on Monday), but it did not cause a spectacular upsurge in the stock exchanges.
The draft of the law on the issue of the program, which is called the 2008 Economic Emergency Stabilization Act, will be voted on by the House of Representatives for the first time on Monday, then at the Senate.
President George W. Bush spoke to Pelosi on Saturday evening and greeted the White House.
“We are satisfied with the progress made at night and appreciate the efforts of both parties to stabilize our financial markets and protect our economy,” White House spokesman Tony Fratto said in a statement.
By the way, the program deviates from the Bush administration’s original probation, which would have meant that the 700-billion-dollar dollars would have been spent one-to-one on the purchase of low-traffic securities.
In the first round, up to a maximum of half of the 700-billion-dollar can be dispossessed with 250 billion forints to be used immediately after Congress is expected to vote on the matter on the matter, from which the Treasur Treasury Treasury will buy low-rated securities, and an additional USD 100 billion will depend on the president’s decision.
The program will be auditioned by a separate body, which will also be attended by the President of the Federal Reserve.
Resumption of the other side of the 700 billion depends on a new resolution of Congress.
According to the law, the Treasury Department can apply combined tactics: you can buy bad loans, mortgages and mortgage-backed securities at the same time, as well as other types of “popoisoning” loans at the same time.
The purchase can be carried out directly through auctions, officials of the ministry said.
The legislation allows the companies concerned to participate in an insurance program, where the Ministry of Finance guarantees the means of concern, for which the companies will pay a fee corresponding to the expected damage.
Henry Paulson said the law would provide the necessary tools in such a way as to save up to 700 billion dollars for the urgent needs of the U.S. financial system, the widespread purchase of bad assets, their insurance, or the avoidance of the systemic risk associated with the collapse of a large financial institution.
“We will take the necessary steps to be ready to enshrine the law as soon as it is signed,” he added.
On the other hand, the bosses of the banks in distress and waiting for state assistance can expect to limit their exit money, which the overseas slang simply calls a golden parachute.
Restrictions on salaries only take place in certain cases, in addition, the limits depend on how the Ministry of Finance provides assistance.
Restriction essentially means that if the ministry buys in the framework of an auction, an additional tax will be levied on the golden parachute, unless the levitated bank manager is retiring.
At the time of the direct purchase of bad loans, the government will intervene aggressively in order to ensure that management money is not exaggerated.
It is part of the image that the existing golden parachute contracts do not change, the restriction applies only to the new ones in order to encourage participation in the programme.
“It’s the first time in U.S. history that there will be a legal restriction on the remuneration of CEOs,” commented Barney Frank, Chairman of the House Committee on Financial Services.
In addition, in the banks participating in the program, the state acquires a right to accompanies, so the risk of taxpayers is diminished, as they can finally win the transaction as soon as the economy and the rescued bank stand on its feet.
The draft law stipulates that over the next five years, the president will have to submit to Congress a proposal that will provide taxpayers to recover expected losses from the financial sector.
Of course, there were people on both sides who expressed their doubts about the plan.
“That’s not what the U.S. citizens have asked us for, meaning they don’t protect taxpayers’ dollars, Republican Congressman Darrell Issa said.
Ohio Democrat Mary Kaptur said: “The measures have been urged by those responsible for the collapse of Wall Street.
Industrial prices continue to rise
The domestic sales prices of the industry increased by 0.1 percent in August 2008 compared to the previous month and by 12.9 percent compared to August 2007.
The prices of industrial export sales were 1.1 percent higher than in July 2008, and they decreased by 3.9 percent compared to the previous year.
As a result of domestic and export price changes, industrial producer prices were 0.7 percent higher than in the previous month and 3.2 percent higher than a year earlier.
The domestic sales prices of the industry only slightly increased by 0.1 percent compared to the previous month.
The domestic sales prices in the manufacturing sectors also slightly increased by 0.1 percent compared to July.
The largest price increase occurred in August 2008 in the leather-grossed manufacturing sector (2.8 per cent), mainly due to the 4.6 per cent monthly price increase in the production of bags and 2.8 per cent, respectively.
The higher price level of plastic and chemical raw materials resulted in a significant monthly increase in the prices of the chemical and product manufacturing sector (1.6 percent).
There has been a fall in the petroleum processing (1.4 per cent), the production of electrical machines (0.9 per cent) and – at a minimum – wood processing (0.1 per cent).
The prices of paper production, publishing and printing activities have not changed.
In the other manufacturing sectors, a price increase of between 0.2 per cent and 0.9 per cent was recorded.
Prices increased by 0.23 percent in the electricity, gas, steam and water supply sector, representing a significant weight in the domestic sales of the industry in August, mainly due to the overriding effect of the official gas price increase carried out from 1 July.
Compared to 12 months, prices in the petroleum processing sector increased the most (32.4 per cent) in terms of domestic sales in the manufacturing sectors (32.4%).
There was an 11.2 per cent price increase in the production of metal-processing products and 11 per cent in the production of food, beverages and tobacco, which represents a determining weight.
Decreasing of prices took place in the production of textiles (2.5 per cent), as well as the production of electric machines and instruments (0.3 per cent).
In the other manufacturing sectors, the price increase was between 0.6 percent and 9.1 percent.
Prices have increased by 10.2 per cent over the past year as a whole.
The prices of the electricity, gas, steam and water supply sectors were 18.7 percent higher than a year earlier.
Domestic sales included the largest monthly price increase (0.7 per cent per cent) among the sector’s intended groups of sectors in August (0.7 per cent).
In the consumer goods manufacturing sectors, prices increased by 0.3 percent, and in energy and re-consumption-generating sectors did not change compared to the previous month.
Compared to the same month of the previous year, the largest price increase occurred in the energy and re-use sectors (16.8 per cent) compared to the same month of the previous year.
In the consumer goods manufacturing sectors, a price increase of 6.8 per cent was recorded, and a price decrease of 1.4 per per cent for capital goods.
The export sales prices of the industry measured in HUF increased by 1.1 percent in August compared to the previous month, and decreased by 3.9 percent in the 12-month compared to 3.9 percent.
Our domestic currency weakened by 1.7 percent against the euro and by 7.1 percent against the dollar in one month, by 7.5 percent compared to the euro and by 15.9 per cent against the dollar compared to August 2007.
