Journal of the German-speaking area
The right-wing camp is untouch-affected, the electorate became more self-sufficient only within the camp – this is how Der Spiegel analyses the poor performance of the CSU in Bavaria.
The Haiders are ready to envelop Austria to prevent a red-black coalition.
Der Spiegel (spiegel.de)
In an online edition of the left-wing German newsmagazine, Franz Walter analyzes the causes of the CSU’s dramatic defiance.
According to the well-known political scientist, the erosion of the CSU began long before Beckstein-Huber duo took office.
Christian socialists have been renewing Bavaria for several decades – and these modern citizens are now turning away from them.
The epocracy of the epoch is also a problem for the whole of Christian democracy, including Angela Merkel.
Failure wasn’t quite surprising for the CSU, but on Sunday a process came to an end that was probable almost exactly ten years ago: an undisputed hegemonia of a state party has ceased.
Ten years ago, in 1998, CSU landed 47.7 per cent, for the first time in the fifties below 50 per cent.
This was recited three years ago in the 2005 Bundestag elections, in the Stoiber era.
The reason for this is the modernization consistently carried out by the CSU in the province that devours its parents.
Sociologists have been predicting this for 40 years, but they have been mistaken at the temporal point of view. However, there could not have been consequences for CSU’s fidelity in secularization, educational achievements, settlements of eastern and northern Germans in the developing regions of Bavaria.
The modernization goes hand in hand with an increase in the possibilities, and its political consequences can also be seen.
The right-wing camp is untouched, only within the camp the electorate became more self-sufficient, more critical, stubborn and more self-sufficient.
It is also striking that in the last few weeks the classical civic groups departed the most away from the CSU; they are free-de-educated, industrialists, graduation graduates and tertiary graduates.
