Járóka: The vicious cycle of poverty and segregation has to be crushed
The European Equal Opportunities Summit in Paris has just started today, hosted by France representing the rotating presiding presidency of the European Union this year.
Lívia Járóka, MEP of the European People’s Party (European People’s Party), drew attention to educational segregation in one of the main speakers of the professional meeting, because this is one of the most serious violations affecting Roma children, and this is also the most serious obstacle to their assertion.
Xavier Bertrand, Minister of Employment and Social Affairs of France and Vladimir Spidla, European Commissioner for Employment, Social Affairs and Equal Opportunities
Among those invited to the deliberation were the representatives of governments, the European social partners and representatives of the European human rights defenders and NGOs in the field of equal opportunities.
Járóka – according to the press office of Fidesz’s MEP’s parliamentary group – warned that, in addition to the discrimination experienced by recruitment, the Roma are excluded from the labour market primarily due to their low educational attainment.
“The vast majority of Roma youngsters do not have a secondary education, and as a result of low-quality elementary education, only an insignificant part can obtain tertiary education.”
Járóka also emphasized that, as a fundamental human right, education plays a key role in enforcing other human rights.
Investing in the early education of Roma children could mean strengthening the economies of the European Member States.
According to the MEP, it is necessary to become aware of these connections in the widest possible way – among Roma and non-Roma.
As soon as possible, the Roma student starts the school, increasing their chances to become productive members of the labour market; they contribute to the national budget through taxes paid on income and consumption.
“Roma must ensure their right to high-education with the right to high-education under the law of their European Union citizenship, and they must be ensured that their low-quality and segregated education can be ensured,” Lívia Járóka said.
According to the MEP, it is necessary to unite the European Union’s goal and action-oriented efforts to immediately accelerate the desegregation processes in schools, for which it is essential to properly enforcing existing and planned anti-discrimination directives.
“The vicious cycle of poverty, segregation and unemployment must be broken, and Roma children should be given the opportunities offered by the democratic space, so that they can contribute to the future of Europe as a full-fledged citizen,” Járóka demanded.
“The European Equal Opportunities Summit to be ensured for all in the spirit of the European Equal Opportunity Year” to be held annually since last year’s meeting in Berlin aims to provide a unique forum to promote equal opportunities and non-discrimination goals set at European and Member State level.
