Occupational Accidents: Napolitano Says Prevention is More Important
There are 2,500 accidents at work in Italy every day, of which three end with death and 27 with permanent invalidity.
According to workplace accident insurance, nearly 1,200 fatalities occurred in the workplace in 2007.
The numbers testify to the seriousness of a phenomenon that is still one of the most common causes of death and almost twice as many victims as homicide.
For the 58th time, Anmil is organising for the 58th time in all counties in Italy, traditionally held on the 2nd Sunday of October, to draw the attention of institutions, civil society organisations and mass communication to this dramatic problem.
“Concerning data communicated by Anmil, as well as the tragic events of the past few days, show that prevention plays an extremely important role in accidents at work,” Prime Minister Giorgio Napolitano told Pietro Mercandelli.
The head of state sincerely praised the “percessive commitment of the association to prevention at the workplace, the protection of the interests of workers who have suffered an accident, to help the family members of the victims, and to make the public sufficiency”.
“Nature indifferent and sincere indifferent outrage is caused by such tragedies, which, by its scale, attract the interests of the media and the general public, but in reality we are confronted with dramatic, re-repeating cases every day.”
Napolitano openly admits: “We are undoubted with a lack of resources, the financing of education and information is crucial.
Initiative initiatives within the school, which aim to enlighten young people on the verge of entry into the world of work, are of paramount importance.
It is our duty to maintain an interest in the phenomenon, not to allow attention to the societal importance of the issue, and we need more effective legislation that need to be enforced.
Gianfranco Fini, for his part, emphasized, “the serious social significance of accidents in the workplace that ends with death, and the institutions are demanded to put an end as soon as possible.”
“Financial Accident Accidents at work shows a slight improvement, but the number of such deaths is still incredibly high,” said Pietro Mercandelli, president of Anmil.
“We don’t want this declining trend to be coupled with declining attention because the biggest tragedies take place just as attention fades.”
“We believe that, at the general level, we are still failing to overcome the phenomenon, which could be solved through a complex programme of action.
“In our opinion, there is a need for stricter supervision and control, Mercandelli stressed, as well as a more pronounced intervention in the field of workforce training, starting from school education.”
“More efforts are also needed to inform the general public and make it susceptible,” he added.
“What is most needed, however, is a more responsible employer environment in the face of a phenomenon that poses a societal problem.”
Mercandelli then criticized “the gradual deterioration of the compensation safety net for the victims of accidents at work”, as well as the “association to which the system has drifted in recent years.”
“The goal, therefore, is to reimburse the safety provided to the workers, because the fate of those suffering from an accident should not depend on the arbitrary and sometimes dividing measures of the legislators.”
