The elite is in danger
The Phenomenon was noticeable a few days ago, when the House of Representatives didn’t vote in favor of a 700-billion-billion-dollar bailout package tabled by the White House: a significant number of U.S. Americans were more interested and are still interested in Punising Wall Street rather than rescuing the economy.
Yesterday’s New York Times also confirms that the current crisis has led to a break within the Republican Party, which shows an increasing division between the traditional, subdued East coastal attitude that supports federal institutions and business circles, and an ever-increasing new stance against Washington and the world in central and southern states.
This direction was critically manifested in the parliamentary vote mentioned in the introduction.
However, the above event is only one of the significant changes predicted by the economic rearrangement that is still going on foreshadowing or letting be seen, not only in the United States, but in the entire Western world, and perhaps beyond.
It seems that the crisis in the U.S. and in the United States and in Europe is only accelerating the phenomena that have been stagnating for a long-lasting time that will solidify and will finally come to light.
The first such phenomenon is re-education, bringing back the dual category of state sovereignty into the public consciousness in connection with a kind of renationalization of the economy.
The crisis affects banking-financial institutions that, although they have a close relationship with the rest of the rest of the world, are carried primarily and fairly extensively by people and families living in a single-member state.
This, in turn, poses a direct political problem for those in the government: how to respond to the difficulties and protests of the people who are otherwise voters.
Thus, in economic terms, the crisis appears to be worldwide, but in a political sense it is almost exclusively national.
It seems that political internationalism is essentially stuck outside the coastline: the European Union, which, navigating between different interests and different national strategies, seems to be stranded between different interests and different national strategies.
As a result, everyone expects individual national governments to intervene, the bailout package, the resuscitation and the new rules, but most importantly the opening of the funds: since only the individual states have economic resources, a financial stockpile that may be able to put things in order.
They also have the means and the power to use them: under certain circumstances, they have two invaluable resources that are unlikely to be disposed of by any other market or any international organization.
Moreover, the new emphasis placed by the crisis on nation-states is in complete harmony with the fact that the recent economic growth attaches great importance to a factor that is closely linked to this element: this is territoriality.
Paradoxically, while we were convinced that we had already entered the abstract world of increasingly sophisticated technology, and we were convinced that the global financial world was destined to rule the world, we suddenly had to realize that our future depended more and more on such abstract things such as water, fruit and oil.
From things that can be transported from one side of the planet to the other, they are entirely related to a demarcated location, an area.
So those who dominate the area have far more trump cards than those who, unfortunately, were born in a place where there is no harvest, there is no oil and no water.
In doing so, I would like to switch to the second novelty, suggesting a kind of fault line.
Anyone who declares that it is an area, a state or a government necessarily also says politics, in other words leadership.
From this point of view, it seems that the economic crisis produces two interrogative phenomena:
On the one hand, an objective recognizing the need for credible leadership, for those who can take events in their hands.
On the other hand, the current leadership, in particular, brings a complete loss of credibility for business leaders, who have proved to be completely unsatisfactory and have proved that there is still a lot of vague territory ahead of them.
Together, the two are of even deeper importance and, in a sense, more disturbing: a sense of loss of credit, impatience, inadequacy, and separation, which affects the entire leadership elite in more and more Western countries.
Therefore, it seems that a broader circle of the population’s belief that the social elite’s time has expired. It has expired before their deeds, their experiences, lifestyles, their culture, in a complex sense of the word have become their own. With this, the social elite is becoming more and more alienated from the opinion of the majority.
In other words, the need for the leadership in a veiled form has a diversified but strong delegitimation movement against the ruling class and the traditional political system. This is manifested in the unforeseen “journabulations” of the electorate, or the sudden changes in the public mood in recent times. For example, Conservatives come to power in Austria and Bajorország, the collision of pro-political unity in Great Great Britain, or perhaps the victory of the American congressmen’s of the Italian parliament.
It seems that the traditional elite, together with their parties, their programs, and might even say that, together with their habits, press and renowned intellectuals, they are becoming less and less able to represent what has been going on for some time in the deeper strata of Western society.
Contemplating “the imminent rebellion of the masses” would be an exaggeration, to be frankly exaggerated.
It doesn’t hurt to keep your eyes open at all.
