Students are suffering from expensive rents, a room can be up to 900 euros
The market for accommodation for university students will not be affected by the crisis.
According to the latest survey of the Sunia Tenants’ Union, a student can pay up to 900 euros for a one-bed room in Milan, 700 in Florence, 650 in Naples and 600 in Rome.
“The phenomenon has now reached an astonishing extent that – due to the huge total costs of families – excludes a wide range of students from the opportunity to study,” explains Laura Mariani, head of Sunia’s study agency. “In these cities, the high demand from students has distorted the market to such an extent that a general price increase process has begun against local residents.”
In the majority of cases, real estate bids also contain a series of irregularities: unregistered contracts with unregistered contracts that do not have a rent restriction, with a related sublance.
According to Sunia, an extraordinary measure would be needed to maximise prices by revising the tax system.
The details of the survey show that a single bed will be available for 450 euros in the vicinity of Brianza in Milan; the price of a one-man room (Bande Nere area) extends from an average of 800 euros ( Lambiate, Udine and Fiera), but it can also reach 900 euros (Vittoria area).
One bed in Florence costs an average of 350/400 euros, in one room about 700 euros.
In Bologna, where the majority of the students live in a neighborhood close to the university, it costs 250/280 euros if it is a double room, 370-500 euros when it comes to a one-bed room.
In Rome in areas close to central universities (San Lorenzo, Piazza Bologna), they charge about 600 euros for a one-man bed and 450 for a double bed.
It is slightly cheaper (550 euros for a one-person room) the prices around the other universities (Ostiense and Cinecitt).
In the sub-urban periphery you can save something: it costs 300 euros for a bed and a single-member single room in Prenestina, Centocelle and other similar districts.
In Naples they charge 300/450 euros for a bed, for one room from 400 to 600 euros. The price is the highest around Policlinico, Vomero and Colli Aminei.
You have to pay 250/350 euros for a bed in Bari and at least 350 for a one-bed room.
In smaller towns, the prices are lower in absolute terms, but they have a greater weight within the urban budget.
In cities such as Perugia, where non-equivalent pupils often look for accommodation, there is another disorder: the rent requested from foreign students is about 25 to 30 percent higher than for Italian tenants.
Farewell good old Cinema Paradiso, in the cities the time of the old movie halls has come to an end
Traditional cinemas are closing in favor of the Multiplexes.
“It’s a city where there’s no cinema, it’s a blind city, it’s like a face without eyes.”
The photographs taken by director Giuseppe Tornatore effectively present everything that takes place in the Italian big cities but also in the rural regions.
Dismantling of cinemas – the appearance of large, multiplied cinemas in the suburbs and, as a result, the atrophiing of the unparalleled ones in the city centers – seems inevitable and irreversible, but at the same time it bears all the characteristics of a real cultural crime.
“It’s like a friend who’s going away, it’s an acquaintance with whom we’ve shared our journey and a variety of experiences,” says Cinema Paradiso’s director. Twenty years ago, the film, which twenty years ago marked an Oscar award and the Grand Prix of the Cannes Film Festival, tells the lyrical story of a small-town movie, the craving, after a movie experience that might be lost forever.
The data provided by Anec, the largest association of the operators, is clear: in the last five years 316 traditional film theaters have been closed. The biggest losses may have been recorded by cinemas in the big cities: 10 movie theaters have been closed in Florence, 9 in Milan, Naples, Bologna and Rome, 6 in Turin and 5 in Palermo.
Emilia-Romagna is at the forefront among the most affected regions, which has lost 82 single-room cinemas, followed by Tuscany (44) and Lombardy (41).
And that the responsibility of multiple extremists for their disappearance proves nothing better than the highest number of newly opened multi-plature cinemas in this three regions of Italy: in the last five years it has risen by 195 in Lombardy, 81 in Tuscany and Emilia-Romagna.
“The number of units in Italy has increased irrationally,” says Anec President Paolo Protti. “It’s okay to build new installations, but we also need to ensure the survival of existing ones, because when an urban movie theater closes, not all passengers are willing to go into the multiplexes.
Not to mention unpatented financial funds intended to support the operators: the overdue debt of the state is estimated at around EUR 10 million.
From an ethical and moral point of point of view, it is a disgrace that the government fails to comply with existing laws.
“We would need 16 to 18 million euros a year to sustain the market.”
This is the first Italian city to resolve the problem in order to protect and revitalize one-room cinemas in the interior and the city, through an agreement between the local government and Anec.
The provisions would include tax allowances granted to the operators, as well as the benefits granted to the public to facilitate accessibility: viewers will be able to drive into restricted zones and park at certain parking lots at preferential tariffs.
“I like this initiative,” says Giuseppe Tornatore, “and I hope that other cities, even with the introduction of other measures, will follow the example of Bologna.
But it’s also certain for the traditional cinemas to be able to cope with the rule of the multiplexes, they have to improve the quality of sound and image, because today’s audience is much more demanding.
Preserving the traditional cinema has always been an important issue for me: Closing Supercinema in Baghheria, where I spent my childhood and young life, gave me the spark that caused me to do Cinema Paradiso, even though I’ve been pondering it for 10 years.
“I’ve been living in Rome for 25 years, so when I’m going before the locked Rialto or the Quirinale at the via Nazionale so I don’t see the black-faceted posters, I turn my head.
Tight twirms between politics and 'ndrangheta, the mayor of Gioia Tauro has been Arrested
“The mayor of Gioia Tauro advocated the interests of the Piromalli clan in municipal decisions.”
Serious allegations have been made against the mayor and deputy mayor Giorgio Dal Torrione and Rosario Schiavone and Rosarno mayor Carlo Martelli, who were arrested this morning on charges of external involvement in a criminal organization.
Gioacchino Piromalli, a senior mediator between the clan and public administration, as well as a lawyer nephew with the same name as the boss, had already been convicted of participating in a mafia-like crime syndicate and fined 10 million euros.
“Opportunity of all legal and moral rules, as well as that of good taste,” writes U.S. Prosecutors. The two local governments have declared their intention to pay an advisory fee to lawyer Piromalli.
“Thus the clan would have formally had access to the City Hall, which would have given us another opportunity to control and direct public administration, which is already significant.
Agazio Loiero, the chairman of the General Assembly of the Calabrian region, does not hide the fact that there is “a number of collusions between the local institutions and the organized underworld in the region”: there are areas where the mafia’s proliferation is stronger than elsewhere, where collusion is almost unavoidable.
However, many local politicians are resisting 'ndrangheta pressure, they're the heroes to stay clean."
Arrangements this morning were a follow-up between the events in July, when the police announced that it had succeeded in beheading the Piromalli clan.
At the end of April, local politicians were acquitted of their mandate to manage the local government. Gioia Tauro’s local government committee was disbanded once in 1991 due to suspected entanglements between organized crime and local government.
Giorgio Dal Torrione, a Christian Democrat mayor who ruled during the disbanding, came to the head of the centre-left coalition in the second round in 2006.
He shouted an outburst when the County Hall ordered the dissolution of the municipal committee.
He summoned Marco Minnini to court and accuse him of politically devising everything. However, the court explained in the arrest warrant signed this morning that the Piromalli clan dared to demand a change in the A3 project.
The eavesdropping ordered against Dal Torrione reveals that “it was naive to comply with the demands of an extensive contractor representing an extensive group known close to the Piromalli family and subcritle the municipality’s decisions to the clan.”
The energy comes from the waves, and the big ones are coming.
An inexhaustible source of inexhaustible energy accounting for 70 per cent of the planet’s surface and is just being explored by major international energy companies.
Waves, sea streams, tides, are all a source of renewable energy on which giants such as Chevron, who are now trying to put their hands on.
For a hundred years they have been researching the opportunity to gain energy from the movements of the sea. In the '70s, at the time of the first oil crunch, research has intensified.
Around 1980, when the price of petroleum fell, the idea of exploiting marine energy was set aside, but today the demand for environmental protection and the resumption of the price of crude oil gives a new impetus to the sector.
London’s World Energy Council (WEC) estimates in 2007 that 15 per cent of the world’s electricity needs could be covered by installations utilizing the fluctuations.
That’s twice what nuclear power plants currently produce.
In addition, the currents and the tides would still be there.
The giants are moving.
Enersis was the first company to be committed to offshore energy and signed an agreement on a power plant to be built in northern Portugal in 2005.
It looked like a revolutionary move when the signature was announced.
It’s been two years since then, and more and more companies are opting for offshore energy, including those who have so far been committed supporters of traditional energy.
“Chavron is studying the technologies utilizing the energy of the oceans and is considering working with us,” a spokesman for an associate business for the U.S. oil giant told the Wall Street Journal.
The cooperation in question relates to the acquittal permit issued by the U.S. Energy Commission to launch an Alaska project for the utilisation of marine energy.
It’s no small issue, especially considering that the U.S. is producing oil in Alaska, and President Bush has recently launched a plan for further drilling.
In addition to Chevron, Verdant Power Inc. in New York and Ocean Power in New Jersey are on the path of unravelable energy from the sea.
The technology needs to be developed.
According to the experts, it is still a rudimentary technology for the time being, also because the energy from the sea does not alleviate doubts about the environmental impact.
Pre-existing installations, such as those of Portugal or Scotland, are all small in size and therefore do not cause too many problems with regard to the ecosystem.
On the other hand, in order to be able to generate energy on a larger scale, installations whose environmental impact is also more important.
There’s a variety of possibilities: Ocean Power, for example, uses an interconnected buoy system.
Shots generated by the waves and the buoy cheques are converted into hydraulic pressure by pistons and cylinders.
The pressure rotates a turbine that feeds a generator.
The electrical electricity is transferred to the mainland on cables under the sea.
There are already four facilities of a similar nature in the planning phase, which are only waiting for the federal permit. Some of the energy produced would be used in a military facility.
Verdant Power, on the other hand, has already started the power generation of a shopping mall and a parking lot with six submarine turbines in New York’s East River Strait.
At tide, the movement of the water rotates the turbine shovels, and this rotary movement feeds the generator.
Verdant said there is a long list of partners who are only waiting to be licensed for the use of energy.
Tramp after the surgedies.
At the moment, special attention should be paid to the opportunities that can be exploited through tides.
Whilst a tide is more predictable than the wind, the sun, or the waves, it can therefore be regarded as a more reliable source of energy.
New York, Maine and Alaska, as well as the other coastal states, spent 7.5 million dollars in 2008 to happily to take advantage of the tide of energy, which is expected to increase to 35 million in 2009.
